    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Expressions-of-Interest--Indigenous-Employment-Opportunities-at-the-University-of-Melbourne_JR-006839": {
      "title": "Expressions of Interest: Indigenous Employment Opportunities at the University of Melbourne",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-04\nFull time\n\nThe University of Melbourne is inviting Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates to submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) for current and future opportunities across both professional and academic positions. Whether you are just starting your career or seeking the next step in your professional or academic journey, we encourage you to express your interest in joining our vibrant and diverse community. You may express interest in the following professional areas: Finance | Call Centre & Customer Service | Student Support Services | HR, Recruitment & Culture | Legal | Fundraising | Administration & Office Support | Management Roles | Strategy, Culture & Analytics | Information & Communications Technology | Marketing & Communications You may express interest in the following academic roles across all faculties: Tutor | Research Assistant | Research Fellow | Lecturer | Associate Professor Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as these positions are exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic).  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfillment, including:  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance, including Cultural Leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program with access to Indigenous counsellors for Indigenous staff Dedicated and ongoing support from Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team A vibrant and active Indigenous Staff Network, providing opportunities for connection and professional development For more information check out our benefits page!  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. We believe the diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing reasonable adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. For assistance, please contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Contact details for other enquiries are located at the bottom of this page. Find more information for applicants requiring reasonable adjustments. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. outlines our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and students.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au for further information and support.  Our Indigenous strategy, Murmuk Djerring outlines our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and students.   Please note : This role requires current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available.   A Working with Children Check (WWCC) is required for all positions. If successful, we'll guide you through the WWCC application process during onboarding and reimburse the cost. Join Us and unlock your career potential!   If this role is right for you, please apply with the following documents:  Resume   Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience Proof of Aboriginality Please note: As part of the application, you’ll be asked to share your preferences for the types of roles and levels of experience that best match your background. For recruitment queries, email Olivia korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Include the Position Number and Job Title in the subject line. Please do not send applications to this email address."
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Early-Childhood-Educator-s---Teacher-s--Casual-Positions_JR-016427": {
      "title": "Early Childhood Educators & Teachers, Casual Positions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-07\n\nPlease apply via this link: https://careers.giighire.com/universityofmelbourne/29027?source=link DO NOT APPLY VIA THE UOM CAREERS PAGE Role type: Casual positions Department : Student and Scholarly services Support high-quality early learning / collaborate in inclusive, research-informed practice / join a respected University childcare service Apply now to contribute your expertise in early childhood education across our vibrant University children’s centres. Investing in you – health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity You will support high-quality early childhood programs across our University centres, grounded in evidence-based pedagogy. You will collaborate with experienced colleagues to create inclusive, play-based learning that strengthens every child’s wellbeing. You will gain varied experience supporting different age groups, rooms, and services within a child-safe organisation.   What you will deliver: Plan, implement, and evaluate play-based learning programs aligned with EYLF, VEYLDF, and service philosophies. Document children’s learning, development, and wellbeing to inform individual planning and continuous programme improvement. Maintain safe, inclusive environments through active supervision, risk management, and adherence to all regulatory requirements. Build strong, respectful relationships with children, families, and colleagues to support belonging and collaborative practice. Collaborate with room leaders and teams to reflect on practice and support service quality improvement.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold an approved Early Childhood degree and current WWCC or VIT registration. Demonstrate sound knowledge of NQF, National Law and Regulations, and Victorian Child Safe Standards. Bring proven experience delivering high-quality education and care using EYLF, VEYLDF, and evidence-based pedagogies. Create inclusive, stimulating environments that promote children’s agency, participation, and strong sense of belonging. Apply diligent risk management, active supervision, and child-safe practices in dynamic early childhood settings. Adapt flexibly across rooms, age groups, and teams while maintaining professional, collaborative conduct.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Student and Scholarly Services Student and Scholarly Services supports students and staff from first enquiry through to graduation and beyond. Early Childhood Education Services operates three high-quality University children’s centres, delivering evidence-based programmes for infants to preschoolers. You will join a collaborative, research-informed team committed to child safety, inclusion, and continuous quality improvement.   Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants  We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials: Please ensure to apply through this link We will NOT be accepting applications through the UOM Careers Page You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience to the link provided as part of the application process. Please apply via this link: https://careers.giighire.com/universityofmelbourne/29027?source=link DO NOT APPLY VIA THE UOM CAREERS PAGE   Position Description: Early Childhood Educator’s & Teacher’s - Casual Positions.docx Early Childhood Educator’s & Teacher’s - Casual Positions.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Lecturer--Indigenous-Social-Equity-and-Leadership---Expression-of-Interest_JR-014395": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership - Expression of Interest",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-10\nFull time\n\nThe University of Melbourne is inviting Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates to submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) for a lecturer, Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership position on a full-time continuing basis. This opportunity is offered at Academic Level B, with a salary range of $124,656–$148,023 per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic).   Your next career opportunity:   You will design, teach and assess postgraduate subjects in Social Change Leadership for diverse experienced Fellows. You will advance research on Indigenous social change while supporting Fellows’ community-led projects and long-term leadership journeys. You will collaborate across the University and global Atlantic Fellows network to grow your academic and impact profile.  What you will deliver:   Design, deliver and coordinate engaging postgraduate subjects, using culturally grounded, flexible teaching approaches for adult learners.  Supervise and support Fellows to develop rigorous social change project proposals with strong community partnerships and ethical foundations.  Conduct and publish high-quality research on Indigenous social change and leadership, securing external funding where appropriate.  Contribute to AFSE’s research, seminars and public engagement, representing AFSE locally, nationally and internationally.  Build and sustain collaborations with Indigenous communities, partners and University colleagues to strengthen AFSE’s teaching and impact.    You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a doctoral qualification, or be near completion, in a field related to Indigenous social change.  Demonstrate experience co-designing curriculum that embeds Indigenous knowledges, cultural safety principles and decolonial teaching practices.  Show a strong record, or clear potential, in research, publication and collaborative project development.  Exhibit excellence in teaching adult learners, including experienced leaders, using inclusive and reflexive pedagogical approaches.  Work effectively within small, interdisciplinary teams, contributing constructively to programme development and shared strategic priorities.  Engage confidently with Indigenous communities and partners, nurturing ethical, long-term relationships that support social change.  Your new team:   Atlantic Fellows for Social Equity You will join the Atlantic Fellows for Social Equity, a collaborative programme advancing Indigenous and Pacific-led social change. AFSE combines a rich Foundation year of fellowship and postgraduate study while nurturing a lifelong community of senior Fellows. The team works closely with communities, partners and a global Atlantic Fellows network to amplify impact. You will collaborate with committed colleagues across the University, including the Faculty of Education, benefiting from strong mentoring and development opportunities.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.  Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.  Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our  Indigenous staff page  to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at  oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit  \"apply with us\"  to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application. Want to know more?   For queries related to this expression of interest, please contact Program Director, Dr. Yvonne Ualesi at yvonne.ualesi@unimelb.edu.au      Position Description: PD_JR-014395 EOI Lecturer Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Associate-Professor-in-Periodontics_JR-009367-1": {
      "title": "Professor in Periodontics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.8 FTE) to Full-time; Continuing Faculty:  Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences School:  Melbourne Dental School Salary: Level D: AUD $183,859 - $202,556 p.a.              Level E: AUD $236,824 p.a.              plus 17% super and clinical loading of AUD $13,494 p.a. International leadership in specialist Periodontics / Shape a nationally benchmarked Doctor of Clinical Dentistry (DCD) Periodontics program / Drive impactful translational research Join us to lead specialist Periodontics education, research and clinical innovation that advances oral health globally. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead the Doctor of Clinical Dentistry (DCD) Periodontics program, integrating specialist clinical training, cutting-edge research and curriculum innovation. You will shape national and international standards in Periodontics while mentoring the next generation of specialist clinicians. What you will deliver:   Lead the design, delivery and evaluation of the DCD Periodontics curriculum and assessment across clinical and academic components. Provide high-quality clinical supervision and safe patient care at RDHM, modelling evidence-based, multidisciplinary specialist Periodontics practice. Establish and lead an internationally recognised research program in Periodontics, securing competitive funding and collaborative partnerships. Supervise and mentor higher degree and postgraduate research students, building research capacity across the discipline and School. Provide strategic leadership in national curriculum frameworks, accreditation standards and professional guidelines for specialist Periodontics. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold and maintain current or clearly eligible specialist AHPRA registration in Periodontics, without limiting conditions or restrictions. Possess a completed PhD in Periodontics or a closely related field, with strong academic credentials. Demonstrate strategic leadership in specialist clinical teaching, including curriculum design, assessment, benchmarking and educational innovation. Show a sustained record of high-quality research, including competitive grant success and impactful translational outcomes. Bring extensive experience supervising higher degree and postgraduate research students through to successful thesis completion. Engage confidently with national and international professional bodies, accreditation panels and multidisciplinary clinical partners. For further information please refer to the attached Candidate Information Pack .  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – “Melbourne Dental School”   You will join the Melbourne Dental School, a leader in specialist dental education and translational research. Working closely with the Royal Dental Hospital of Melbourne, you will help deliver world-class patient-centred clinical training. You will collaborate in a values-driven Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences, committed to Advancing Health 2030. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au   Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Professor Petros Papagerakis or Professor Ivan Darby at the details listed in the position description. For questions related to our recruitment process email Shabon Nawabi at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Friday 9th October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   Candidate Information Pack - Professor in Periodontics.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Senior-Partnership-and-Engagement-Manager--MEC_JR-018267": {
      "title": "Senior Partnership and Engagement Manager, MEC",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (2 years)   Department: Melbourne Entrepreneurial Centre, Melbourne Enterprise   Salary: UOM 9 - $146,049 - $151,951 p.a. plus 17% super   Senior leadership role in a growing entrepreneurial centre / build strategic corporate and philanthropic partnerships / shape MEC 2030 impact   Join MEC to lead partnerships, community engagement and events that fuel Melbourne’s entrepreneurial ecosystem and venture success.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.       The University of Melbourne     We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   In this senior role, you will lead MEC’s partnerships, community and events functions to drive sustainable growth.   You will cultivate corporate and philanthropic partners while energising a vibrant network of students, alumni, mentors and innovators.   What you will deliver:   Lead MEC’s commercial and philanthropic partnerships strategy, pipeline and portfolio to grow revenue and strategic relationships.   Build and activate MEC’s community, alumni and mentor networks to support programs and entrepreneurial engagement.   Oversee a high-quality events program that showcases MEC, strengthens partnerships and deepens community connections.   Provide inclusive leadership, clear direction and coaching to partnerships and engagement staff within a growing team.   Monitor performance, budgets and risk, delivering regular reports on revenue, engagement metrics and partnership impact.     You may be a great fit if you:   Demonstrate a strong record securing corporate and philanthropic partnerships with measurable revenue and relationship outcomes.   Apply commercial acumen to build and manage partnership pipelines, forecasts and stakeholder reporting in complex organisations.   Design and deliver stakeholder, community or alumni engagement programs that deepen long-term participation and advocacy.   Lead and coach professional staff, fostering collaborative, high-performance cultures aligned with shared goals.   Plan and deliver impactful events, from intimate partner engagements to large-scale showcases, with excellent participant experiences.   Engage confidently with entrepreneurship and start-up ecosystems, using data and insights to improve programs and partnerships.     What we offer you!     We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:     Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being     Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance  and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company     Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program       For more information check out our benefits page!     Your new team:   Melbourne Entrepreneurial Centre (MEC) is the University’s hub for entrepreneurship, supporting students, researchers and alumni to build ventures.   MEC sits within Melbourne Enterprise and Melbourne Research and Enterprise, connecting deeply with faculties and industry partners.   Based at Melbourne Connect, you will join a collaborative team scaling programs, partnerships, community and impact.   Be Yourself     The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb .   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process .   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants     We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .    Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.      Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Monica Bulla-Valencia   at monicab@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au     For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').      Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: ​ Sunday 11 October 2026 - 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:     PD_JR-018267 Senior Partnership &amp; Engagement Manager, MEC.pdf PD_JR-018267 Senior Partnership &amp; Engagement Manager, MEC.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Werribee/Equine-Veterinary-Nurse_JR-017945-1": {
      "title": "Equine Veterinary Nurse",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Continuing Faculty:  Science Department/School:  Melbourne Veterinary School – Werribee Equine Centre Salary:  UoM 4: $80,400 - $85,328 p.a. plus 17% super Renowned equine teaching hospital environment / varied hospital and ambulatory caseload / strong focus on education and growth Step into a leading equine teaching hospital and grow your nursing career supporting complex, meaningful cases. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   As an Equine Veterinary Nurse, you will provide high quality care in hospital and ambulatory settings. You will support specialist clinicians, mentor DVM students and contribute to a leading equine teaching hospital. What you will deliver:   Provide high quality equine nursing care, including horse handling, restraint, treatment support and ongoing inpatient husbandry. Assist veterinarians with diagnostics, surgery, anaesthesia, and emergency care, ensuring thorough preparation and post procedure monitoring. Maintain clinical equipment, stocks, and records, upholding rigorous biosecurity, infection control and workplace safety standards. Support delivery of ambulatory services by preparing vehicles, equipment and providing nursing assistance during farm visits. Guide DVM students in practical equine nursing, horse handling and professional behaviour within a supportive teaching environment. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a Diploma or Certificate IV in Veterinary Nursing, or equivalent experience, ideally with equine focus. Bring proven equine veterinary nursing and horse handling experience within a clinical practice or hospital environment. Demonstrate strong organisational skills, with ability to manage competing priorities and follow detailed clinical protocols. Apply sound knowledge of animal welfare, infection control, biosecurity and safe large animal handling practices. Use veterinary practice software and animal care databases confidently to support accurate records and billing. Value working in a collaborative teaching hospital, supporting veterinarians and mentoring veterinary students. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Melbourne Veterinary School Melbourne Veterinary School, in the Faculty of Science, is a highly ranked leader in veterinary education. The Werribee Equine Centre delivers specialist referral, primary care and ambulatory services as a leading equine teaching hospital. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Carina Wickens (Details in Position Description) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 19 October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-017945 Equine Veterinary Nurse_PD.pdf JR-017945 Equine Veterinary Nurse_PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Academic-Positions--Level-B--C-or-D--Education---Research-Indigenous-Law-and-Justice-Hub_JR-018041-1": {
      "title": "Academic Positions (Level B, C or D) Education & Research Indigenous Law and Justice Hub",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time, Continuing Faculty: Melbourne Law School / Indigenous Law and Justice Hub Salary: UoM B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super UoM C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super UoM D - $183,859 - $202,556 p.a. plus 17% super  Champion Indigenous legal scholarship and advocacy / shape culturally safe curricula and research partnerships / collaborate with First Nations scholars Apply now to help redefine legal education and research through Indigenous knowledge, leadership and community partnerships. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic).     The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   Melbourne Law School invites applications for an academic position within the Indigenous Law and Justice Hub. The level of appointment will be commensurate with the successful applicant’s qualifications, experience and academic profile. The successful applicant will contribute to the School’s teaching, research, engagement, leadership and service, bringing expertise in any area of law relevant to the work of the Indigenous Law and Justice Hub. What you will deliver:   Teaching in the Melbourne Juris Doctor, Melbourne Law Masters and, where appropriate, undergraduate Breadth programs. Contributing to curriculum and subject development, particularly in relation to law and Indigenous peoples. Supervising coursework and/or higher degree research students, as appropriate to level and experience. Undertaking high-quality, nationally and internationally significant legal research and publishing in leading outlets. Contributing to research projects, grant activity and knowledge exchange. Supporting an engaging, inclusive and high-quality student learning experience. Building relationships and partnerships through the Indigenous Law and Justice Hub and contributing to community and professional engagement. Contributing to the collegial life, leadership and service of Melbourne Law School and the University, with more senior appointments expected to make a significant contribution to Indigenous strategy and cultural capability. You may be a great fit if you:   A strong academic background in law and relevant postgraduate qualifications, including a PhD or equivalent (or equivalent professional experience where applicable). A developing, established or internationally recognised record of legal research and publication, commensurate with level. Teaching experience and a commitment to high-quality legal education and student outcomes. The capacity to contribute to research, engagement, leadership and service. A commitment to collegiality, diversity and inclusion, and maintaining a culturally safe workplace. Relevant professional experience, networks and/or expertise in legal education and educational technologies will be highly regarded. Applicants should identify the academic level for which they are applying and demonstrate their suitability against the relevant criteria. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Melbourne Law School The Indigenous Law and Justice Hub brings together Indigenous leaders, legal scholars and communities to advance justice. Based within Melbourne Law School, the Hub embeds Indigenous knowledges across teaching, research, engagement and student support. You will join a values‑driven, empathetic and collaborative team committed to cultural safety and community‑led priorities.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Nicole Watson at nicole.watson.1@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 18 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: MLS Academic Recruitment 2026 Indigenous PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Research-Fellow---Senior-Research-Fellow_JR-018549": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow / Research Fellow / Senior Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time, Fixed-term until November 2027 (part-time considered) Faculty: Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology Salary: Level A - $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super  Level B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Level C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super  Work on secure containment for AI agents atop seL4 / collaborate with world-leading formal methods experts / shape next-generation AI-assisted verification tools Apply now to help build safer AI systems and advanced verification methods within a dynamic international research team. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will join an international project securing AI agents using formally verified seL4-based reference monitors. You will develop AI-assisted verification techniques and contribute to large-scale Isabelle/HOL proof engineering. You will also gain experience supervising students and collaborating across academia, government and industry. What you will deliver:   Advance research on secure AI containment by designing and verifying seL4-based reference monitors and mechanisms. Develop AI-assisted verification methods and AI proof agents to support large-scale Isabelle/HOL proof maintenance and evolution. Publish high-quality research, present findings at leading conferences, and contribute to collaborative project reports and deliverables. Supervise and mentor students and junior researchers, fostering capability in formal methods and automated reasoning. Collaborate with national and international partners, and contribute to grant proposals, outreach and broader departmental activities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in formal methods, automated reasoning, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrate a strong publication record in leading venues, commensurate with experience and opportunity. Bring expertise in areas such as interactive theorem proving, formal verification, security foundations or AI-assisted reasoning. Exhibit excellent analytical, problem-solving and data management skills, with meticulous research record keeping. Communicate complex concepts clearly to technical and non-technical audiences, both in writing and in person. Thrive in collaborative, multi-disciplinary teams and show initiative in leading projects or supervising students. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology You will join the Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology’s vibrant formal methods and security community. Our researchers collaborate across computing, AI and systems to build trustworthy, high-assurance technologies with real-world impact. You will work closely with leading academics and international partners on the Agentic seL4 project. Benefit from strong mentoring, seminar programmes and access to advanced tools, infrastructure and collaborative networks. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. Visa sponsorship is possible; however preference will be given to candidates who can start immediately. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Toby Murray at toby.murray@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Saturday October 24 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Descriptions Word: Postdoctoral Research Fellow (Toby Murray) PD.docx PDF: Postdoctoral Research Fellow (Toby Murray) PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow---Clinical-Diagnostic-Translation-Research_JR-018362": {
      "title": "Research Fellow – Clinical Diagnostic Translation Research",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full Time, Fixed Term for 12 months Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry & Health Sciences Department/ School:  Department of Infectious Diseases Salary: Level A - $87,266 - $118,416 or Level B $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Multidisciplinary infectious diseases diagnostics research / real-world clinical and self-collection implementation / national and international field study leadership Join our team to shape future STI diagnostics through impactful translational research and strong clinical partnerships. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead clinical diagnostic implementation studies that improve STI detection and care in real-world settings. You will design and deliver multi-site trials, engaging clinicians, laboratories and health services across Australia and internationally. You will gain mentoring within a collaborative team, building a strong track record in translational infectious diseases research. What you will deliver:   Lead design and delivery of diagnostic implementation studies, including multi-site STI point-of-care trial expansion and monitoring. Develop study protocols, ethics submissions and regulatory documentation, ensuring compliance and robust governance across all projects. Coordinate participant recruitment, data collection and qualitative and quantitative analysis with clinical and laboratory collaborators. Train and support site staff in research procedures and diagnostic testing, providing remote oversight of study conduct. Mentor students and junior researchers, contribute to manuscripts and presentations, and participate actively in laboratory meetings.   You may be a great fit if:   Hold a PhD in a relevant discipline or equivalent experience in implementation or infectious diseases research. Bring experience co-designing and delivering research with health services, diagnostic laboratories, clinicians and consumers. Demonstrate expertise implementing medical technologies, ideally diagnostics, within complex multi-stakeholder clinical and laboratory environments. Possess a strong track record in clinical research, including ethics applications, data management and mixed-methods analysis. Excel at managing competing priorities while mentoring junior colleagues and maintaining meticulous project documentation and records. Commit to high scientific and ethical standards and to building effective collaborations with diverse external partners. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – You will join the Pasricha Laboratory within the Department of Infectious Diseases at the Doherty Institute. Our team brings together clinician-scientists, basic scientists, epidemiologists, and public health professionals. We focus on antimicrobial resistance, sexually transmissible infections, HIV, and emerging infections. You will benefit from a collaborative, mentoring-oriented culture that supports impactful translational diagnostics research and career development. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au   Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Shivani Pasricha by emailing Shivani.pasricha@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 8 October. 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018362 Research Fellow – Clinical Diagnostic Translation Research PD.pdf JR-018362 Research Fellow – Clinical Diagnostic Translation Research PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Werribee/Clinical-Skills-Support-Nurse_JR-018146": {
      "title": "Clinical Skills Support Nurse",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed Term until 31/12/2027 Faculty: Science School: Melbourne Veterinary School Salary: UoM 4: $80,400 – $85,328 p.a. plus 17% super Pivotal role shaping DVM clinical skills / varied multi-species work across leading veterinary sites / supportive expert team Bring your veterinary nursing expertise to empower DVM students and strengthen clinical teaching across Melbourne Veterinary School. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will support DVM students to build essential clinical skills through supervised rotations and practical teaching activities. You will work across leading clinical sites, guiding safe animal handling and nursing practices in multi-species settings. You will join an experienced, collaborative team that values innovation in veterinary education and continuous professional growth. What you will deliver:   Provide practical support to DVM students during clinical rotations at Greencross Veterinary Hospital and The Lost Dogs’ Home. Assemble and support clinical skills training, assessments and simulation-based teaching across key veterinary species and procedures. Prepare and maintain anaesthesia, surgical teaching and animal handling resources, ensuring equipment, models and stock meet best-practice standards. Uphold animal care ethics, patient safety and infection-control requirements, supporting veterinarians and students before and after surgery. Build strong relationships with clinical partners, contribute to service improvements, and participate in governance, meetings and training. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a Certificate IV in Veterinary Nursing or equivalent, with proven experience in clinical veterinary practice. Demonstrate confidence in handling cats, dogs and horses, and assisting safely with a range of clinical procedures. Support training and supervision of students or staff, preferably within veterinary clinical skills or teaching environments. Manage clinical spaces, equipment, medicines and consumables, maintaining accurate patient records and documentation to high standards. Apply Occupational Health and Safety practices in clinical settings, prioritising animal welfare, student safety and regulatory compliance. Embrace working across multiple sites and rotations, adapting to changing demands and collaborating within an experienced multidisciplinary team. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Melbourne Veterinary School The Melbourne Veterinary School, part of the Faculty of Science, is Australia’s number one ranked veterinary school. We deliver Victoria’s only professional entry veterinary medicine program, grounded in strong industry partnerships and impactful clinical education. Within the School, the clinical teaching support team offers over 160 years of combined, multi-species experience. You will join a collaborative, versatile group that values curiosity, sharing ideas and continually improving veterinary teaching and patient care.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Maddi Taper (details in PD) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 18 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018146 Clinical Skills Support Nurse_PD.pdf JR-018146 Clinical Skills Support Nurse_PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Casual-Tutors--Faculty-of-Business-and-Economics_JR-018769": {
      "title": "Casual Tutors, Faculty of Business and Economics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\n\nTutors are a critical part of teaching teams in the Faculty of Business and Economics. Under the guidance of a Subject Coordinator, Tutors will deliver weekly classes and contribute significantly to students' academic success and satisfaction with university life. The Faculty are seeking Tutors in the departments of Accounting, Economics, Finance, and Management & Marketing. Responsibilities include: - Preparing and delivering high quality, student-centered small group learning experiences - Providing student consultations - Marking assignments and exams About you: You have excellent written and verbal communication skills and a passion for teaching in accounting, economics, finance, management and/or marketing disciplines. You are able to navigate subject websites and teaching technology with ease and work collegially with others. You will also have: - An Honours or postgraduate level qualification in a relevant discipline and/or equivalent relevant academic or professional or practice-based experience and expertise - A current Victorian Working With Children Check (WWCC) by the commencement of the teaching period For further information please refer to the attached PD. Apply today! Applications MUST be submitted via the Casual Tutor Recruitment System (CTRS). Please visit https://ctrs.app.unimelb.edu.au/ctrs/ to apply. Applications submitted using the Apply Now button at the top of this advertisement will NOT   be reviewed. Applications Close: Sunday 8th of November at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description Word: 2027 FBE Tutor.docx PDF: 2027 FBE Tutor.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Online-Educator-in-Artificial-Intelligence-or-Cyber-Security_JR-018590": {
      "title": "Online Educator in Artificial Intelligence or Cyber Security",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.4 FTE); Fixed-term for 24 months  Faculty: Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology Department/ School:  School of Computing and Information Systems Salary: Academic Level A $87,266 - $118,416 (pro rata) p.a. plus 17% super  Academic Level B $124,656 – $148,023 (pro rata) p.a. plus 17% super   Shape future cyber security or artificial intelligence experts / Flexible remote teaching role / Join top-ranked Computing & Information Systems School   Apply now to become a key player in delivering cutting-edge cyber security or artificial intelligence education in a flexible, online environment  Investing in you - benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   As an Online Educator in the Master of Cyber Security or Master of Artificial Intelligence programmes, you'll play a crucial role in shaping the next generation of cyber security or artificial intelligence professionals. You'll have the opportunity to facilitate student learning, provide guidance and support, and contribute to the success of this innovative online programme. This flexible, remote role allows you to balance your commitments while making a significant impact in the field of cyber security education or artificial intelligence. What you will deliver:   Deliver Exceptional Student Experience: Facilitate engaging learning activities and provide empathetic support to students throughout their online learning journey.  Assess and Provide Feedback: Undertake assessment tasks, including marking and providing high-quality, timely feedback to students.  Contribute to Teaching Excellence: Prepare and deliver weekly live online workshops, and participate in professional development to enhance teaching practices.  Collaborate and Innovate: Work with the academic team to contribute to the improvement of teaching and learning practices in the online cyber security or artificial intelligence programmes.    You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a Master's degree or PhD in an IT-related or cognate discipline.  Have demonstrated experience in teaching cyber security or artificial intelligence subjects at a university level.  Possess a strong understanding of key cyber security or artificial intelligence concepts and disciplines.  Have excellent written and verbal communication skills.  Be able to work effectively both autonomously and as part of a team.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of Computing and Information Systems The School of Computing & Information Systems (CIS) undertakes research and teaching across a range of information technology disciplines including Software Engineering, Information Systems, and Computer Science. CIS is the most highly ranked School of Computing and Information Systems in Australia according to all major rankings (THE, QS, ARWU). It offers a comprehensive range of IT and IS courses at all levels, including offerings in science, engineering, and business, and is at the forefront of computing research in Australia and internationally with close links to major computing research initiatives, including Melbourne Bioinformatics, CSL, The Cremorne Digital Hub and CSIRO’s DATA61. The School’s aim is to attract and retain outstanding staff in order to maintain a leading research and teaching profile. We have an existing highly successful research team in the area of the appointment, a large number of PhD students, and a substantial cohort of graduate students in our coursework Master programs. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Shangqi Lai at shangqi.lai@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 18 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   Word: Online Educator in AI or Cyber Security_PD.docx PDF: Online Educator in AI or Cyber Security_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Graduate-Research-Examinations-Officer_JR-018404-1": {
      "title": "Graduate Research Examinations Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nPosition Number: JR-018404 Role type: Full-time; Fixed-term to May 2027  Department/School: Student and Scholarly Services – Graduate Research (Student Administration)  Salary: UOM 5: AUD $86,943 - $99,864 p.a. plus 17% super   Specialist role in graduate research examinations / collaborate across the University and with external examiners / drive service and process improvement   Apply now to play a pivotal role in delivering timely, high-quality outcomes for graduate researchers.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   In this role, you will manage graduate research examinations from submission through to final completion. You will support candidates, academics and examiners while improving processes and reducing delays across the examinations cycle. What you will deliver:   Support the end-to-end graduate research examinations process, monitoring progress and intervening early to minimise delays.   Interpret and apply policies, advising candidates, examiners and staff on examination requirements, outcomes and available options.   Maintain accurate student records, ensuring enrolment, examination and completion data are processed promptly and correctly.   Support implementation of viva examinations, system enhancements and process improvements within the Graduate Research team.   Contribute positively to team priorities, projects and service reviews to enhance experience for graduate researchers. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a degree-level qualification coupled with relevant administrative or higher education experience.   Demonstrate exceptional attention to detail, accuracy and confidence using complex administrative and student systems.   Manage competing priorities effectively, organising workload to meet deadlines in a busy, deadline-driven environment.   Adapt readily to changing policies, processes and systems, contributing constructively to implementation and continuous improvement.   Collaborate proactively within a close-knit team to share knowledge, balance workloads and maximise service efficiency   Communicate clearly and diplomatically with diverse stakeholders, maintaining confidentiality in sensitive or complex situations.   Bring knowledge of graduate research administration and TechnologyOne, or eagerness to quickly develop this expertise.   For further information, please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team You will join the Graduate Research team within Student and Scholarly Services, a collaborative, service-focused group. The team partners with faculties, external examiners and central services to support every stage of graduate research candidature. You will help deliver consistent, high-quality experiences for graduate researchers while contributing to ongoing process and systems improvements . Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.   Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team, via korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Mary Makris at m.makris@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien Nguyen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').  Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: Sunday, 18th October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: PD_JR-018404 Graduate Research Examinations Officer.docx PD_JR-018404 Graduate Research Examinations Officer.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-in-Gender-Studies_JR-019042": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Gender Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.8 FTE); Fixed term until 31 Dec 2029 Faculty: Faculty of Arts Department/ School:  School of Social and Political Sciences Salary: Level B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Shape cutting-edge Trans and Queer Studies scholarship / inspire diverse Gender Studies students / join a values-driven social sciences school Apply now to lead inclusive Gender Studies teaching and research focused on Trans and Queer Studies scholarship. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will help shape transformative Gender Studies curricula, with a special focus on Trans and Queer Studies scholarship. You will develop your research profile, collaborate across disciplines, and contribute to an inclusive, socially engaged School culture. What you will deliver:   Coordinate and deliver engaging undergraduate and honours subjects in Gender Studies, including high-enrolment classes and team teaching. Design inclusive, innovative curricula and technology-enhanced learning resources that support an increasingly diverse student cohort. Undertake high-quality research in Trans and Queer Studies, producing peer-reviewed publications and seeking competitive grant funding. Supervise honours, graduate coursework and research higher degree students, fostering collaborative and multidisciplinary projects. Contribute to School and Faculty leadership, committees, and external engagement, including national and international partnerships. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed PhD in Gender Studies or a closely related discipline. Specialise in Trans and Queer Studies with a research focus that complements existing Gender Studies strengths. Demonstrate excellence in tertiary teaching, including coordinating subjects, developing curricula and delivering high-enrolment classes. Supervise honours and postgraduate coursework theses, and contribute to research higher degree supervision. Maintain a strong, developing publication record and show capacity to secure competitive research grants and consultancies. Apply inclusive pedagogies and collegial behaviours that support diverse students and embody Faculty of Arts values. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of Social and Political Sciences You will join the School of Social and Political Sciences, a leading hub for innovative social science research. Our multidisciplinary community spans Anthropology, Development Studies, Gender Studies, Criminology, Political Science and Sociology. We are driven by values of intellectual freedom, integrity, social justice, diversity and environmental sustainability. Here, you will collaborate with supportive colleagues to redefine the world and change what matters. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Assoc. Prof. Kalissa Alexeyeff at k.alexeyeff@unimelb.edu. k.alexeyeff@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 1 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   Word: Lecturer in Gender Studies_PD.docx PDF: Lecturer in Gender Studies_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Thomas-and-Margaret-Ruth-McArthur-Research-Fellow_JR-018964": {
      "title": "Thomas and Margaret Ruth McArthur Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Fixed-term (to 31 December 2029) Faculty: Arts Department/School:  School of Social and Political Sciences Salary: Level A $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super  Prestigious social sciences postdoctoral fellowship / Dedicated research funding up to $10,000 p.a. / Mentoring in a world-class interdisciplinary School Apply now to deepen your research profile and shape debates in anthropology and the social sciences. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   In this prestigious fellowship, you will drive independent research in anthropology or cognate social sciences disciplines. You will strengthen your publication profile, pursue external funding, and collaborate within a vibrant interdisciplinary School. What you will deliver:   Lead high-quality independent research in anthropology or cognate social sciences, aligned with School and Faculty priorities. Produce scholarly publications and conference presentations that enhance the School's research reputation. Develop and submit competitive external research grant applications, supported by dedicated fellowship research funds. Contribute to the School's academic life through limited supervision, guest lecturing, and collegial service activities. Maintain safe work practices and comply with OH&S and EH&S responsibilities relevant to your research. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in Anthropology or a cognate social sciences discipline. Demonstrate capacity for independent scholarship and impactful research in the nominated field. Bring a publication record in refereed journals or publishing houses, relative to opportunity. Communicate clearly and confidently, with strong written and verbal skills for diverse audiences. Collaborate effectively and contribute collegially to an inclusive Faculty of Arts community. Align with Faculty values of diversity, inclusion, and high professional standards. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of Social and Political Sciences Within the Faculty of Arts, the School of Social and Political Sciences is a leader in social research. The School brings together Anthropology, Development Studies, Criminology, Gender Studies, Political Science, and Sociology. You will join a collegial community committed to impactful scholarship, public engagement, and distinctive social science education. Senior academics will support your development through mentoring, collaboration, and opportunities to build an enduring research profile. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Associate Professor Jeff Garmany at jeff.garmany@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 1 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: Word: The Thomas and Margaret Ruth McArthur Research Fellow.docx PDF: The Thomas and Margaret Ruth McArthur Research Fellow.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/International-Partnerships-Adviser_JR-019055": {
      "title": "International Partnerships Adviser",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nole type: Full-time; Fixed term until 31 January 2028  Department Chancellery – Global, Culture and Engagement  Salary: UOM 7: $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super   Strategic global partnerships portfolio / Focus on Northeast Asia / Work closely with senior University leaders  Apply now to lead strategic international initiatives that strengthen the University’s partnerships, influence and global reach.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   You will support strategic international partnerships, providing analysis, advice and coordination that advance the University’s global ambitions.  You will develop relationships across Northeast Asia and beyond, identifying education and research collaboration opportunities and innovative partnership models.  What you will deliver:   Produce intelligence briefs, market analyses and reports to inform international engagement and partnership decisions in Northeast Asia and other regions.  Contribute to business cases, proposals and implementation plans for new institutional partnerships and strategic international projects.  Support development and delivery of regional strategies, education and research collaborations, and innovative transnational education initiatives.  Coordinate international visits, delegations and events, ensuring effective programming, stakeholder engagement and timely follow-up actions.  Maintain high-quality partnership management processes, including relationship tracking, governance support and continuous improvement of systems and frameworks.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a relevant degree, or bring an equivalent combination of education, training and international engagement experience.  Demonstrate strong written communication skills, including preparing briefs, reports, correspondence and presentations for senior stakeholders.  Manage multiple projects and administrative activities concurrently, exercising sound judgement, diplomacy, discretion and careful attention to detail.  Build effective relationships with international partners, understanding cross-cultural sensitivities and engaging confidently across diverse contexts.  Apply experience in process development and improvement to enhance partnership management, reporting and evaluation practices.  Bring experience in higher education, work in another country, or fluency in an Asian language.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team:   Chancellery Global, Culture and Engagement leads the University’s international strategy, partnerships and global profile.  Guided by the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Global, the team shapes regional strategies and advances Advancing Melbourne Globally 2030.  You will join talented colleagues who collaborate across faculties and Chancellery, engaging with governments, partners and senior leaders.  We offer a dynamic, outward-facing environment where your insights help deliver meaningful international impact for the University.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter addressing Essential Selection Criteria 1, 2, 3 found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria   Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Julie Fam at j.fam@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​ Sunday 18 October 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    PD_JR-019055 International Partnerships Adviser .pdf PD_JR-019055 International Partnerships Adviser .pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/AURIN-Associate-Software-Engineer_JR-018050": {
      "title": "AURIN Associate Software Engineer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term Faculty: Architecture, Building and Planning Department/School: Australian Urban Research Infrastructure Network (AURIN) Salary: AUD $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% super  Entry-level software role in national research infrastructure / build modern cloud, data and API skills / support better urban decisions undefined Join AURIN to grow your engineering career while building platforms that support impactful city and regional research. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   As Associate Software Engineer, you will build and enhance AURIN’s cloud-first platforms for urban research. You will develop technical skills across modern languages, data systems and cloud tools while collaborating with experts. What you will deliver:   Contribute to designing, developing, testing and maintaining software components that power AURIN’s digital research infrastructure platforms. Implement integrations and RESTful services that connect data, analytical tools and visualisation systems for researchers and policymakers. Work within a team-based squad, pairing with senior engineers to solve technical challenges and deliver sprint objectives. Apply best-practice version control, CI/CD and containerisation workflows to support reliable, scalable deployments. Participate in documentation, support, and continuous improvement activities, ensuring systems remain usable, secure and well-governed. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold entry-level experience across the software development lifecycle, from requirements gathering through implementation, testing and maintenance. Possess foundational skills in languages such as Java, Python and JavaScript for application development. Understand web servers and databases, including technologies like Apache or Nginx, MongoDB and PostgreSQL. Work effectively within small collaborative teams and balance competing priorities to meet agreed deadlines. Use modern collaborative engineering practices, including git-based workflows, CI/CD, agile delivery and containerised deployments. Demonstrate motivation to up-skill through mentoring, self-directed learning and formal professional development opportunities. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team AURIN sits within the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning, delivering nationally significant urban research infrastructure. You will join a team of engineers, data specialists and researchers enabling evidence-based decisions for cities and regions. The team values collaboration, learning and innovation, providing mentoring, modern tools and opportunities to grow your technical capabilities. By contributing to AURIN’s platforms, you will support research on sustainability, housing, transport and urban resilience across Australia. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Brett Verity at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Position Description:   JR-018050 AURIN Associate Software Engineer PD.docx JR-018050 AURIN Associate Software Engineer PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Financial-Aid-Advisor_JR-018524": {
      "title": "Financial Aid Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed term for 12 months Department: Student and Scholarly Services Salary: UOM 5 - $86,943 - $99,864 p.a. plus 17% super Meaningful impact on students’ financial wellbeing / shape evolving financial aid and arrival services / thrive in a supportive team Apply now to help students navigate financial assistance options, manage key arrival services, and enhance a vital support offering. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will support students seeking financial assistance and coordinate arrival services for new students.    You will process interest-free loans and hardship grants, manage student enquiries, and connect students with wider wellbeing support.    You will also contribute to service redesign and continuous improvement in a collaborative and supportive team. What you will deliver: Assess student loan and hardship grant applications, ensuring accurate eligibility decisions and timely processing of payments. Engage directly with students via appointments and enquiries, triaging cases, escalating appropriately, and referring to specialist services. Oversee day-to-day delivery of arrival services, liaising with external contractors and stakeholders to ensure smooth operations. Maintain accurate service information and templates across channels, including Teams, OneCRM and student-facing web content. Contribute to service improvement initiatives, supporting digital uplifts, process redesigns, and reviews that enhance student experience.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold a tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline, or equivalent education and experience in student services. Demonstrate experience in university student services or lending environments, working with financial hardship and wellbeing contexts. Manage time-critical workloads and competing demands with strong organisational skills and attention to detail. Operate confidently within protocols, policies and compliance requirements, applying privacy and confidentiality in record-keeping. Use CRM systems such as Salesforce, plus Microsoft Teams, Word, Excel and Outlook in daily work. Contribute proactively to a small, collaborative team, adapting to service redesign and continuous improvement activities.   For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – Student and Scholarly Services Student and Scholarly Services sits within Chancellery and provides end-to-end administration, wellbeing and scholarly support. Within this area, the Financial Aid team supports students through loans, hardship grants and coordinated arrival services. You will join a lean, positive team that values collaboration, flexibility, innovation and enhancing the student experience. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Alex Spezza at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday the 18th of October 2026 at 11:55 PM; AEDT. Position Description: JR-018524 - Financial Aid Advisor - PD.pdf JR-018524 - Financial Aid Advisor - PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Lecturer--Nursing_JR-018635": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Nursing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nRole type:  0.5-1FTE; Fixed term for 12 Months Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/School: Department of Nursing; Melbourne School of Health Sciences Salary: $124,656 - $148,023 (pro-rata for part time) plus 17% super  Lead entry-to-practice and postgraduate mental health nursing education / shape evidence-based curriculum and simulation / partner with leading clinical services Contribute to the Department’s commitment to fostering excellent mental health nursing education that advances practice, supports students and deepens impactful clinical partnerships. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will coordinate and teach mental health nursing across postgraduate programs, shaping high-quality, evidence-based learning experiences. You will connect classroom, simulation and clinical practice, working closely with students and expert clinical partners. You will also contribute to scholarship in learning and teaching within a supportive, research-informed academic community. What you will deliver:   Coordinate and teach mental health nursing subjects across entry-to-practice and postgraduate programs using contemporary blended and online approaches. Develop, review and enhance curriculum and simulation activities grounded in evidence-based mental health nursing practice. Translate research and scholarship into innovative teaching that improves student learning and professional readiness. Build and maintain strong relationships with clinical partners and contribute expertise to professional and community activities. Engage actively in departmental and school committees, supporting inter-professional learning and strategic educational initiatives.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold current AHPRA registration as a Registered Nurse and a master’s degree in nursing. Possess postgraduate qualifications and recent clinical experience in mental health nursing practice. Demonstrate advanced therapeutic skills, including expertise in child safety standards and family violence responses. Bring experience delivering mental health nursing education and coordinating programs or subjects within nursing. Maintain an established scholarship profile in learning and teaching, including publications or conference presentations. Communicate and collaborate effectively with students, colleagues and external stakeholders to support high-quality education. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team You will join the Department of Nursing within the Melbourne School of Health Sciences at the Parkville campus. The team is committed to excellence in nursing education, research and clinical partnership, with strong connections to leading health services. Colleagues value collaboration, compassion, respect, integrity and accountability, and champion diversity and inclusion. You will work alongside experienced educators and researchers who are dedicated to improving mental health care and enhancing student success. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and relevant experience and addressing the Key Selection Criteria (KSC) outlined in the Position Description in one document . Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Fiona Newell at fnewall@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Please note - Applications will be reviewed as they are received. Applications close: Monday 19th October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018635 Lecturer, Nursing - PD.docx JR-018635 Lecturer, Nursing - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Curator--Rare-Books_JR-018643-1": {
      "title": "Curator, Rare Books",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nPosition Number: JR-018643 Role type: Full-time; Continuing Location: Parkville Department: Archives and Special Collections (Student and Scholarly Services) Salary: UOM 7: AUD $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super Steward an internationally significant rare books collection and join the team leading  creative public engagement initiatives Apply now to curate a collection that powers research, teaching innovation and inspires public engagement at Melbourne. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will curate and interpret the University’s Rare Books Collection for diverse audiences. You will help drive object-based learning, research and exhibitions, while enhancing access through contributing to cataloguing, digitisation and outreach. You will shape how this extraordinary collection supports innovative scholarship across disciplines. What you will deliver: Provide oversight for physical access, storage, handling, movement and loans, ensuring best practice care of rare materials. Develop exhibitions and interpretive programs, underpinned by collection-based research, to broaden discovery and engagement. Design and deliver object-based learning and collection literacy programs embedded within curriculum and e-learning environments. Contribute to digitisation, catalogue enrichment and rights management to improve discovery and responsible access to materials. Advise on acquisitions, donations and deaccessions, ensuring compliant trust fund use and robust collection documentation.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold postgraduate qualifications in Museum or Curatorial Studies, or equivalent experience with historical or cultural collections. Possess tertiary qualifications in humanities or Library Science, or an equivalent blend of education and experience. Bring deep knowledge of rare and antiquarian books, manuscripts and print history, including collecting and publishing contexts. Demonstrate experience curating exhibitions and access programs, and interpreting complex material for diverse and non-specialist audiences. Apply advanced skills in cataloguing, condition reporting, collection management systems and safe handling of cultural heritage materials. Collaborate confidently with academics, donors, booksellers and professional staff within large, multifaceted institutions or libraries. Embrace emerging digital, metadata and learning technologies to enhance collection discovery, teaching integration and online engagement. For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team   You will join Archives and Special Collections, a specialist department within Student and Scholarly Services. The team develops, cares for and shares outstanding archives and special collections for teaching, learning and research. You will work with collegial experts who value collaboration, innovation, service excellence and inclusive, community-focused engagement. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Jon Buckingham via jon.buckingham@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien Nguyen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Monday 26th October at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: PD_JR-018643 Curator, Rare Books.docx PD_JR-018643 Curator, Rare Books.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/STEM-Outreach-Programs-Coordinator--STEM-EDGE_JR-018864": {
      "title": "STEM Outreach Programs Coordinator, STEM EDGE",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-Term for 12 months   Faculty: Faculty of Science  Department/ School: Student and Academic Services   Salary: UoM 6: $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% super  Champion equity-focused STEM outreach for girls and gender diverse students / coordinate flagship programs across Science / grow your impact in a supportive, inclusive faculty.  Apply now to share your STEM passion, equity values and personal story while shaping students' futures.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.     The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity   In this identified role, you will coordinate STEM EDGE and contribute to programs that inspire young people to explore opportunities in STEM.  You will coordinate engaging camps, events and mentoring experiences that build confidence, develop skills and connect students with STEM role models.  You’ll be part of a values-driven team committed to equity and improving the representation of women and gender-diverse people in science.  As part of the Faculty’s commitment to improving representation in science, this role is open only to applicants who are women or gender diverse, in line with the special measure provided for under section 12 of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic).   What you will deliver:    Plan, develop and coordinate STEM EDGE, The Science Experience and other outreach programs within the Faculty.  Promote programs, recruit participants and schools, and manage logistics to ensure safe, engaging and inclusive experiences.  Build and maintain relationships with students, families, schools, mentors and University staff to support participation and impact.  Recruit, train and support student mentors and volunteers, fostering community and positive role modelling for participants.  Monitor program outcomes, evaluate and report data, and identify risks and improvements, including child safety requirements  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent combination of education, training and related experience.  Demonstrate passion for STEM education and enthusiasm for engaging school students, especially girls and gender diverse students.  Bring project management skills to deliver programs on time, within budget and with evidenced positive outcomes.  Possess experience working with schools or youth programs and understanding of duty-of-care and child safety responsibilities.  Show awareness of equity issues and barriers facing girls and gender diverse students pursuing STEM pathways.  Demonstrate confidence communicating with varied stakeholders and initiative to host events, workshops and student activities.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program     For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Student and Academic Services   You will join the Faculty of Science’s Student and Academic Services team, dedicated to transformative STEM education. The team leads flagship outreach programs that connect diverse school communities with inspiring scientists and authentic university experiences.   We champion equity, inclusion and belonging, particularly for girls and gender diverse students exploring STEM futures. You will collaborate across schools, institutes and partners, gaining broad networks and support to grow your outreach expertise.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Marie Kinsey at marie.kinsey@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').     Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: 25 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    JR-018864 - STEM Outreach Programs Coordinator, STEM EDGE - PD.docx   JR-018864 - STEM Outreach Programs Coordinator, STEM EDGE - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Software-Engineer_JR-018676": {
      "title": "Research Software Engineer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (2 years) Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/School: Melbourne School of Population and Global Health, Centre for Epidemiology and Biostatistics Salary: UOM 6 - $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% superannuation Shape epidemic forecasting infrastructure across Australia and New Zealand / focus on professional software engineering, not academic publishing / collaborate with leading epidemiologists, statisticians and data scientists Apply now to use your software engineering expertise to deliver robust epidemic analytics tools that inform policy Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity You will design and deliver open-source software that powers national epidemic forecasting and scenario modelling hubs. You will work closely with leading epidemiologists and data scientists, turning complex health data into actionable insights. This professional software engineering role lets you focus on building high-quality tools rather than academic publications. What you will deliver: Plan, design, develop, test and deploy open-source research software that supports ACEFA’s forecasting and scenario modelling hubs. Create modern web interfaces, dashboards and user experiences, integrating secure back-end services, databases and cloud infrastructure. Engage software users and collaborators through code repositories and forums, supporting multi-team participation in epidemic analytics. Implement robust data management, security and confidentiality practices aligned with population health research policies and standards. Produce clear technical documentation, reports and project communications that enable reuse, scalability and ongoing maintenance of tools.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold a bachelor’s degree in computer science, software engineering or a similarly computational discipline. Design and implement high-quality research software using R, Python, JavaScript or comparable data-focused programming languages. Develop modern web interfaces and visualisations using JavaScript frameworks, HTML5, CSS3 and relevant libraries. Apply strong software engineering practices, including Git, testing, continuous integration and high-performance computing skills. Manage project workload effectively, meeting milestones and deadlines while maintaining clean, reproducible and well-documented code. Collaborate within multidisciplinary teams, communicating complex technical concepts clearly to non-technical public health stakeholders.   For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Infectious Disease Dynamics Unit You will join the Infectious Disease Dynamics Unit within the Centre for Epidemiology and Biostatistics. Our team collaborates across universities, government and ARDC partners to tackle pressing infectious disease threats. You will work in a values-driven, multidisciplinary environment that blends data science, public health and impactful research. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Alex Spezza at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 18th of October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: JR-018676 - Research Software Engineer - PD.docx JR-018676 - Research Software Engineer - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Shepparton-Victoria/Deputy-Director--Medical-Student-Education--Echuca_JR-018936-1": {
      "title": "Deputy Director, Medical Student Education- Echuca",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nPart time\n\nRole type:  2x 0.25 FTE positions; Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department:  Department of Rural Health Salary: Level C: $152,695 - $176,065 per annum (pro rata for part-time) p.a. plus 17% super  Shape rural medical education for MD students / build partnerships with regional health services / lead a supportive student culture Join us to lead clinical teaching in regional Victoria and support the next generation of rural doctors. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead and deliver high quality clinical education for MD students based at our Echuca campus. You will shape rural medical training, support student wellbeing, and work with clinicians across the Rural Clinical School. What you will deliver:   Lead implementation and continual improvement of the clinical curriculum for MD students within the Rural Clinical School. Collaborate with Echuca Regional Health and Deputy Directors at other sites to coordinate teaching and student rotations. Provide regular academic and pastoral support, including weekly meetings, to ensure students thrive in regional placements. Deliver high quality clinical teaching, assessment, and course coordination aligned with University policies and standards. Engage in research and scholarly activity, including supervising MD research students and contributing to rural health projects.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a medical qualification recognised in Australia, plus college fellowship or equivalent clinically relevant postgraduate degree. Bring extensive experience in clinical teaching, medical education, and curriculum delivery for medical students. Maintain active clinical practice in the Wangaratta region and demonstrate national recognition within your clinical specialty. Demonstrate strong course coordination, organisational, and administrative skills within a university or teaching hospital environment. Build collaborative relationships with students, clinicians, and health services, providing high quality academic and pastoral support. Show familiarity with Victorian regional hospitals and an interest in rural health and rural medical education. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – The Department of Rural Health sits within the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences and delivers rural clinical education across multiple regional sites, including Echuca, Ballarat, Shepparton and Wangaratta. The team works closely with partner health services to provide high quality medical training that strengthens rural workforces and improves community health outcomes. You will join a supportive, values-driven culture that champions collaboration, compassion, respect, integrity and accountability, and invests in staff development and wellbeing. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Steven Bismire For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 23 October. 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018936 Deputy Director, Medical Student Education- Echuca PD.docx JR-018936 Deputy Director, Medical Student Education- Echuca PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lawyer--MDHS-_JR-018932-1": {
      "title": "Lawyer (MDHS)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing  Department: Legal and Risk, COO Portfolio  Salary: UOM 8 - $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. plus 17% super  Advise on complex health, research and commercial law matters / partner with Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences (MDHS) and Melbourne Research and Enterprise (MRE) leaders / grow versatile in-house legal expertise  Apply now to shape strategic legal outcomes for health, research and education across a leading university.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   You will provide high-quality legal advice on complex health, research and commercial matters across MDHS and MRE. You will work as a trusted partner to senior stakeholders, shaping strategic decisions and managing legal risk.  What you will deliver:   Provide clear, solution-focused legal advice on areas including research, clinical trials, IP, employment, regulation and student placements.  Draft, review and negotiate diverse commercial agreements, including research, technology, licensing and collaboration arrangements.  Build strong partnerships with MDHS and MRE stakeholders, enabling early legal input into key initiatives.  Support Legal and Risk projects, driving process improvements and consistent, efficient legal service delivery.  Manage routine and complex matters independently while collaborating closely with colleagues across the MDHS cluster and the Legal and Risk team.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a law degree, at least two years’ PQE and a current Victorian Practising Certificate.  Bring experience in commercial contracts and one or more of research, clinical trials, IP or digital health.  Demonstrate strong drafting, analytical and negotiation skills, with a focus on practical, solution-oriented advice.  Manage competing priorities and ambiguity while exercising sound judgment about when to escalate or seek guidance.  Build productive relationships with diverse stakeholders and contribute positively to a collaborative, high-performing team.  Maintain high ethical standards, professionalism and confidentiality when dealing with sensitive or complex legal matters.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as  learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team:   Legal Services is the University’s in-house legal team providing strategic legal support across teaching, research and operations. You will join the MDHS cluster, working closely with leaders in medicine, dentistry, health sciences and research. The team champions Service Excellence, One Team and University First, fostering collaboration, learning and continuous improvement.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please use the cover letter and resume template (under Application Document) below to create and outline your experience. Resumes not in the template below will not be accepted.    Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Alex Maschmedt at  amaschmedt@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Ethan Khamly at ethan.khamly@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications Close: ​ Sunday 25th October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Application Document: Template CV - UOM8 Lawyer (MDHS)- 2026.docx   Position Description:   JR-018932 Lawyer (MDHS) PD.pdf   JR-018932 Lawyer (MDHS) PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Senior-Lecturer-Social-Change-and-Education-Policy_JR-019132": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer Social Change and Education Policy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time, fixed term (four years) Faculty: Education Department/School:  Education and Society Salary: Level C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super  Shape education systems through critical policy scholarship / drive social change with impactful research / teach diverse future educators Join a collaborative academic community to advance education policy scholarship and inspire students committed to social justice Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead innovative teaching and research on social change, education policy and governance. You will design and coordinate subjects across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, including online and blended delivery. You will collaborate with colleagues and partners to influence educational futures locally, nationally and internationally. What you will deliver:   Design, deliver and coordinate engaging subjects on social change and education policy across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. Embed Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander education and diverse social justice perspectives throughout curricula and teaching practice. Build a high-quality research programme, securing competitive funding and publishing in leading international outlets. Supervise Graduate Research students and contribute to research projects focused on education policy and social change. Contribute academic leadership and service through committees, professional associations and collaborative partnerships with external stakeholders. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD or research doctorate in education or a closely related discipline. Demonstrate strong expertise in education policy, governance and social change, including contemporary theoretical and policy debates. Show a developing track record of high-quality publications and success in securing competitive research funding. Bring experience designing, coordinating and delivering tertiary subjects, with confidence using digital and blended pedagogies. Provide evidence of effective Graduate Research supervision or co-supervision within relevant fields. Engage collaboratively across disciplines and with external partners, including communities, governments and professional associations. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Faculty of Education Within the Faculty of Education, the School of Education and Society brings together scholars committed to understanding education as a public, relational and ethical endeavour. The team leads impactful research and teaching that informs policy, professional practice and system reform, with a strong focus on equity, inclusion and sustainability. You will join a collegial community that values innovation, partnership and research-led teaching to shape more just educational futures. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Marcia McKenzie at marcia.mckenzie@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 25 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description - Word: JR-019132_Senior Lecturer, Social Change and Education Policy_PD.docx PDF: JR-019132_Senior Lecturer, Social Change and Education Policy_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-Health-and-Physical-Education_JR-019063": {
      "title": "Lecturer Health and Physical Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time; Fixed term to 30 November 2027 Faculty: Education Department/School:  School of Education and Society Salary: Level B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Advance excellence in teaching, research, and leadership within the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport disciplines, contributing meaningfully to both the Faculty of Education and broader academic community.  Engage with innovative pedagogies and interdisciplinary scholarship, and shape the future of education in a dynamic and supportive environment.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will be a member of an academic team within the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport (HOPES) specialisation in the Faculty of Education, and play a pivotal role in advancing teaching, scholarship, engagement, and leadership while also contributing more broadly to the fields of teaching and teacher education. You will coordinate and deliver subjects at both undergraduate and graduate levels, with a particular emphasis on the Faculty of Education’s undergraduate breadth subjects and graduate initial teacher education programmes. What you will deliver:   Preparing and delivering diverse teaching modalities, developing and reviewing curricula, and coordinating subject administration including supervision of sessional staff.  Assessing student progress through timely feedback, marking, moderation, supporting practical application of theory, and monitoring teaching quality for continuous improvement.  Conducting and disseminating scholarship in the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport (HOPES) field, securing research funding, supervising graduate research students, and engaging in national and international academic forums.  Providing leadership through contributing to professional development, participating in faculty and university committees, supporting colleagues, and engaging with relevant professional and community organisations.    You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD or research doctorate relevant to the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport (HOPES) specialist area with a focus on education and teaching.  Demonstrate experience in course or subject development, subject coordination, and providing specialised support to students on placement.  Maintain a developing research profile with a strong record of publications in leading academic outlets and the ability to contribute to research funding and supervision of graduate research students.  Possess proficiency in digital literacy and familiarity with contemporary digital pedagogies to support innovative research, learning, and teaching practices.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – Faculty of Education The Faculty of Education (FoE) fosters staff productivity, growth and engagement in a collective effort to enrich the contribution that education makes to society. We conduct research and teaching that leads to the transformation of education practice both within and beyond the profession. Faculty of Education stimulates learning that enriches the potential of students from around the world, enabling meaningful careers and profound contributions to communities. We provide research leadership, setting the direction for high-impact, innovative and responsive research that addresses the pressing issues of our time. We lead purposeful engagement with society, sharing our resources and expertise as part of collaborative efforts to build a resilient, equitable and sustainable future.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact John Quay at jquay@unimelb.edu.au . For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: JR-019063_Lecturer, Health and Physical Education_PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-in-Buddhist-Studies-and-Philosophy_JR-018900-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Buddhist Studies and Philosophy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.8 FTE); Fixed-term (3 years); Level B Education-focused Faculty: Arts School:  Historical and Philosophical Studies Salary: Level B: AUD $124,656 – $148,023 p.a. (pro rata) plus 17% super Shape an innovative Buddhist Studies minor / champion inclusive, student-centred teaching / contribute to a leading humanities faculty Apply now to advance Buddhist Studies and Philosophy education while building your research and engagement profile. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead engaging, inclusive teaching in Buddhist Studies and Philosophy across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. You will also build your research profile and partnerships, contributing to SHAPS’ vibrant intellectual and community life. What you will deliver:   Design, coordinate and deliver high-quality subjects for the Buddhist Studies minor and related Philosophy offerings. Innovate curricula, including technology-enhanced learning and cross-disciplinary, student mobility or overseas teaching experiences. Produce high-quality research outputs and actively contribute to seminars, conferences and broader disciplinary networks. Foster inclusive, respectful classrooms that support diverse learners and embed Buddhist philosophical perspectives. Contribute to School and Faculty service through committees, compliance, and ongoing professional development. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed PhD in Philosophy, History, Religious Studies or a cognate discipline focused on Buddhist Studies. Demonstrate a developing track record of high-quality research publications or equivalent scholarly outputs. Deliver excellent, inclusive teaching and lead subject or course development within Buddhist Studies and Philosophy. Coordinate subjects or programmes, including supervising tutors and supporting honours or postgraduate coursework students. Use educational technologies confidently to design engaging learning, assessment and online or blended experiences. Build respectful collegial relationships and external networks, including community or industry engagement where possible. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team SHAPS brings together History, Philosophy, Classics, Archaeology and related disciplines in a vibrant humanities community. You will join colleagues committed to rigorous scholarship, inclusive teaching and rich engagement with local and global partners. As part of the Faculty of Arts, you will help educate critical, ethically minded graduates. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Hannah Gould at hannah.gould@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Petra at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018900 - Lecturer in Buddhist Studies and Philosophy_PD.docx"
