    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Senior-Lecturer--Economics---Econometrics_JR-018940-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer, Economics / Econometrics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time, Continuing Faculty: Faculty of Business and Economics Department/ School:  Department of Economics Salary: Level C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super  World-leading economics and econometrics department / Shape research, teaching and policy impact / Cooperative, well-resourced environment Join a top-ranked department to lead influential research, inspire students, and mentor future economists and econometricians. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We strongly encourage applications from women and people who identify as women The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead high-quality research in economics or econometrics within a globally recognised, impact-driven department. You will teach engaged students, supervise ambitious researchers, and help shape innovative curricula and academic leadership. What you will deliver:   Produce internationally recognised research and actively secure external funding through grants, fellowships and collaborations. Deliver high-quality teaching in specialist and core subjects across undergraduate, honours and graduate programmes. Supervise and mentor honours, masters and PhD students, fostering rigorous research training and academic development. Provide academic leadership through subject coordination, curriculum innovation and contributions to departmental strategy and governance. Engage with government, industry and community partners to translate research insights into policy and practice. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in economics, econometrics or a closely related discipline, completed by commencement. Demonstrate a strong, sustained research record with publications in leading peer-reviewed economics or econometrics journals. Teach effectively in specialist economics or econometrics subjects and contribute to core undergraduate or postgraduate teaching. Secure, or show strong potential to secure, competitive research funding and cooperative policy or industry projects. Provide high-quality supervision and mentoring for honours, masters and PhD students across diverse research topics. Contribute positively to departmental leadership, service, curriculum development and external engagement with government or industry. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Department of Economics You will join the Faculty of Business and Economics, renowned for teaching with an impact, research and industry engagement. Within the Department of Economics, you will collaborate with leading economists, econometricians and actuaries across diverse fields. You will engage with specialised research units and labs, from applied microeconomics to market design and environmental data. Our collaborative, inclusive culture supports excellence, mentoring and strong connections with business, government and the wider community. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Phillip McCalman at econ-jobhire@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 15 January 2027 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   Word: JR-018940_Senior Lecturer in Economics and Econometrics_PD.docx PDF:   JR-018940_Senior Lecturer in Economics and Econometrics_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Fellow-in-Nanocrystal-Synthesis_JR-018907": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Nanocrystal Synthesis",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-Term for 12 months  Faculty: Faculty of Science  Department/ School: School of Chemistry  Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super (PhD entry level $110,319)    Drive nanocrystal innovation for ultra-low-cost solar PV / Collaborate across chemistry and engineering / Build your independent research profile.  Apply now to advance innovative nanocrystal synthesis for ultra-low-cost solar technologies within a supportive, diverse team.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.     The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity   You will lead the synthesis and scale-up of nanocrystals that enable ultra-low-cost solar technologies.  You will collaborate with leading chemists and engineers while developing your independent research profile and leadership skills.  You will also mentor students and contribute to a safe, inclusive and high-performing laboratory environment.  What you will deliver:    Conduct innovative research on nanocrystal synthesis, scale-up and characterisation aligned with ACAP solar PV objectives.  Lead daily operations, safety, and maintenance of the materials synthesis laboratory and specialist equipment.  Supervise and mentor undergraduate and postgraduate researchers, contributing to training, risk assessments and group meetings.  Collaborate with national ACAP partners, preparing reports, publications and presentations on research progress and outcomes.  Develop new project ideas and competitive funding applications to build your independent research trajectory.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a PhD in physical chemistry or materials science and engineering, or an equivalent discipline.  Bring demonstrated experience in nanomaterials synthesis and characterisation, including nanocrystal purification and scale-up.  Demonstrate a strong publication record and ability to write high-quality reports and journal manuscripts.  Manage laboratory-based projects safely, including equipment upkeep, OH&S compliance and risk assessment approval processes.  Collaborate effectively in multidisciplinary teams and engage with industry or professional stakeholders on joint projects.  Show potential to develop independent research directions, secure funding and supervise or mentor student researchers.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program     For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – School of Chemistry   Based in the School of Chemistry within the Faculty of Science, you will join a collegial community. You will work closely with the Australian Centre for Advanced Photovoltaics node and interdisciplinary engineering collaborators. Our shared focus is advancing renewable energy technologies while fostering diversity, inclusion, professional development and research excellence.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Associate Professor Wallace Wong at wwhwong@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').     Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: 29 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    JR-018907 - Research Fellow in Nanocrystal Synthesis - PD.docx JR-018907 - Research Fellow in Nanocrystal Synthesis - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Security-Leader_JR-018634-1": {
      "title": "Research Security Leader",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing  Department: MRE – Office of Research Ethics and Integrity  Salary: UOM 9 - $146,049 - $151,951 p.a. plus 17% super  Strategic research security leadership role / shape compliant global collaborations and protect University reputation / partner with senior academics, regulators and government  Apply now to lead a high-performing research security team and influence secure, responsible research across the University.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.      The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   You will lead a specialist research security team supporting secure, compliant and impactful research collaborations.  You will advise on complex national security, export controls and foreign interference risks across a leading research-intensive university.  You will shape education, guidance and policy that strengthen research integrity and protect the University’s global reputation.  What you will deliver:   Lead and mentor Research Security Advisors to deliver high-quality, timely advice and support to researchers.  Oversee allocation, workflow and prioritisation across the team, balancing reactive requests and proactive compliance initiatives.  Provide expert guidance on complex research security matters, including export controls, sanctions and foreign interference obligations.  Monitor emerging geopolitical, research security and cybersecurity risks, and translate insights into strengthened protections and practices.  Develop and deliver training, briefings and communications, and represent the University in engagements with regulators and sector forums.    You may be a great fit if you:   Hold postgraduate qualifications in a relevant discipline, or equivalent experience in research, security or compliance.  Demonstrate deep knowledge of research security, foreign interference guidance and related legislation affecting the university sector.  Apply advanced analytical thinking and sound judgement to complex, sensitive and ethical research risk scenarios.  Communicate complex regulatory and security concepts clearly through training, briefings, outreach and written guidance.  Lead and develop staff, drive efficient team operations and build strong relationships across diverse stakeholder groups.  Draft regulatory submissions, engage confidently with government and regulators, and remain curious about emerging research security issues.    What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!      Your new team:   Melbourne Research & Enterprise supports researchers to secure funding, translate discoveries and deliver real-world impact. Within this division, the Office of Research Ethics and Integrity leads governance, ethics, integrity and research security frameworks. You will join a collaborative, service-oriented team that partners with academics, professional staff, institutes and government. The team values integrity, responsiveness and continuous improvement, offering rich exposure to complex national security issues and sector-leading practice.   Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Mel Green  at mel.green@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').     Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​ Wednesday 21 October 2026 -11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    PD_JR-018634 Research Security Leader .pdf PD_JR-018634 Research Security Leader .docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Facilities-Specialist-Lead--Peter-Doherty-Institute_JR-017797-1": {
      "title": "Facilities Specialist Lead, Peter Doherty Institute",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing  Department: Campus Operations & Delivery, Enterprise Services Group  Salary: UOM 8 - AUD $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. plus 17% super  Influential facilities management leadership in world-class infectious disease institute / safeguard complex containment facilities / expanding remit including AIID  Apply now to lead critical facilities that enable cutting-edge infectious disease research and care.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   In this role, you will lead the Doherty Engineering Group supporting a world-class infectious diseases institute.  You will oversee complex building services, safeguard high-containment laboratories and shape systems for the future AIID facility.  What you will deliver:   Lead and develop the Doherty Engineering Group, fostering collaboration and high-quality support for diverse bio-research professionals.  Coordinate facilities management, maintenance and engineering activities to support Joint Venture objectives and Institute teaching and research.  Identify and mitigate infrastructure risks, ensuring safety, compliance, business continuity and accurate engineering documentation.  Manage asset monitoring systems, long-term replacement planning and annual operational budgets for building services and equipment.  Oversee consultants, contractors and lease obligations, representing COD in key committees and driving continuous operational improvement.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a degree in engineering or equivalent experience and technical aptitude in facilities management within in a complex building environment.  Lead and contribute to an operationally focussed facilities teams within large, technically complex buildings, ideally health, hospital or laboratory settings.  Apply strong project and contractor management skills, including risk assessment and root cause analysis.  Demonstrate broad technical aptitude across mechanical, electrical, hydraulic and laboratory engineering services and control systems.  Use digital control systems and information management tools confidently to monitor, document and improve building performance.  Have excellent interpersonal and communication skills with a demonstrated ability to engage with a range of building stakeholders.  Manage operational budgets and financial reporting, including building outgoings and cost-saving opportunities.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team:   Campus Operations & Delivery sits within the Enterprise Services Group, supporting over 600 buildings across the University.  At the Peter Doherty Institute, you will join a specialised facilities team enabling infectious disease research and care.  You will collaborate with University and health partners, shaping high-containment infrastructure and future operations for the AIID facility.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Brian Cullen at cullen.b@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​Wednesday 21 October 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:   PD_JR-017797 Facilities Specialist Lead – Peter Doherty Institute.docx  PD_JR-017797 Facilities Specialist Lead – Peter Doherty Institute.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Operations-Manager--Melbourne-National-Security-and-Defence_JR-019056": {
      "title": "Operations Manager, Melbourne National Security and Defence",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (2 years)  Department: Chancellery – Melbourne Research and Enterprise  Salary: UOM 10 - $156,480 p.a. plus 17% super  Institution-wide leadership on national security and defence / shape new governance and operating frameworks / high-visibility senior role  Join us to build a resilient, secure University and lead operational excellence across national security initiatives.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   In this senior role, you will establish and lead the operating framework for Melbourne National Security and Defence (MNSD). Reporting to the Associate Director, Research Collaboration, you will work closely with the MNSD Executive Director and specialist University teams, to support effective governance, risk management and compliance for complex, sensitive activities. You will assist coordinated engagement with government, industry and research partners to strengthen the University's defence capabilities.   What you will deliver:   Provide strategic operational leadership for MNSD, developing and implementing its architecture, operating framework and core activities.  Oversee day-to-day operations, workflows and resourcing across national security and defence initiatives, aligning activity with University priorities.  Lead governance, risk and compliance processes, ensuring robust controls and adherence to DISP and institutional frameworks.  Build strong relationships with senior leaders, academic staff and partners, acting as a trusted operational point of contact.  Drive continuous improvement in service models, reporting, performance measures and ways of working across MNSD and partner teams.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a postgraduate qualification and extensive experience in large-scale project or operations management at corporate level.  Operate effectively at senior management level, cultivating relationships and engaging diverse stakeholders in complex environments.  Demonstrate strong expertise in governance, risk and compliance within large, complex or highly regulated organisations.  Coordinate activity across multiple portfolios or functions, managing interdependencies to achieve whole-of-organisation outcomes.  Produce clear, persuasive operational reports, briefings and advice for governance committees and executive audiences.  Bring understanding of the defence and national security research environment, including associated risks, opportunities and compliance expectations.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team: You will join Melbourne Research and Enterprise within Chancellery, supporting the University’s ambition to translate research excellence into societal benefit.  Melbourne National Security and Defence brings together strategic governance, institutional coordination and partner engagement to strengthen the University’s contribution to national security and defence. It connects expertise across the University, supports priority research capabilities and helps coordinate engagement with government, industry and higher education partners. Working closely with the MNSD Executive Director and reporting to the Associate Director, Research Collaboration, you will help build the operational foundations of this initiative.    Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.   Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   You will need to be eligible to hold an appropriate Australian Government security clearance.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Michael Webb at m.webb@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​ Sunday 18 October 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    PD_JR-019056 Operations Manager, Melbourne National Security and Defence.docx PD_JR-019056 Operations Manager, Melbourne National Security and Defence.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Senior-Advisor--Gender-Based-Violence_JR-017963-1": {
      "title": "Senior Advisor: Gender-Based Violence",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nPosition Number: JR-017963   Role type: Full-time; Continuing  Department/School: Student and Scholarly Services  Salary: UOM 8 - AUD $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. plus 17% super  Specialist trauma-informed role supporting victim survivors / shape University-wide gender-based violence responses / influence safer campus culture  Apply to use your gender-based violence expertise to support our community and strengthen institutional responses.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity   You will provide specialist, trauma-informed support to students and staff impacted by gender-based violence. You will guide colleagues and influence safer practices across the University’s Safer Community Program. You will deepen your expertise applying national codes and risk frameworks in a complex, values-driven setting.   What you will deliver:    Provide trauma-informed responses to disclosures and reports of gender-based violence from students and staff.  Connect impacted community members with specialist services and complaint processes within and external to the University.  Assess risk, escalate urgent or complex matters, and contribute to coordinated safety planning across the University.  Deliver capacity-building workshops and secondary consultations to strengthen colleagues’ confidence responding to gender-based violence.  Maintain high-quality, confidential case records and support continuous improvement of Safer Community Program practices.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a tertiary qualification in Social Work or an equivalent combination of education and experience.  Demonstrate advanced, trauma-informed practice with victim survivors and understanding of support models for perpetrators.  Bring strong knowledge of gender-based violence risk assessment, safety planning and relevant police and court processes.  Understand the National Higher Education Code and university reporting and complaint processes, or quickly learn this context.  Excel at building constructive relationships with diverse stakeholders in large, complex organisations.  Produce clear, sensitive written documentation, including detailed case notes and accurate information management records.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Student and Scholarly Services   You will join Student and Scholarly Services within the Chief Operating Officer Portfolio, a collaborative team that supports students from first enquiry to graduation. The Safer Community Program leads University-wide responses to concerning, threatening and harmful behaviours, partnering closely with faculties and central divisions. You will work with committed professionals who value evidence-informed practice, prevention and early intervention, and who are focused on building a safer, more respectful environment for every member of the University community.   Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description below  Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien Nguyen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: Tuesday, 20th October 2026, at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    PD_JR-017963 Senior Advisor Gender-Based Violence.docx   PD_JR-017963 Senior Advisor Gender-Based Violence.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow-in-Microbial-Fluid-Dynamics_JR-018985": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Microbial Fluid Dynamics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed term (2 years) Faculty: Science Department/School: School of Mathematics and Statistics Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super ARC-funded microbial fluid dynamics role / explore how flows shape microbial behaviour and signalling / join a top applied mathematics group. Apply now to drive pioneering microbial fluid dynamics research and contribute to world-class applied mathematics teaching. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead innovative research on microscale fluid dynamics that explains how flows shape microbial movement and communication. You will combine mathematical modelling, computation and collaboration to produce high-impact publications and support student learning. What you will deliver:   Lead research on microbial fluid dynamics in unconfined and confined flows aligned with the ARC Future Fellowship. Develop and analyse mathematical and computational models of cell motility, navigation and micro-scale chemical interactions. Publish high-quality papers, present at conferences, and contribute to competitive research funding proposals. Teach one Applied Mathematics subject per year or equivalent, and support student supervision and mentoring. Engage with collaborators, participate in School activities, and promote the University’s research profile and values. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed or near-completed PhD in Applied Mathematics or a closely related discipline. Demonstrate a strong research track record in areas aligned with fluid dynamics or cell motility. Bring experience in continuum or agent-based modelling and in developing computational simulations. Collaborate effectively within multidisciplinary teams and contribute positively to a collegial research culture. Communicate research clearly through publications, presentations, and engagement with diverse internal and external audiences. Offer desirable strengths such as high-performance computing, teaching experience or familiarity with experimental biophysics. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of Mathematics and Statistics The School of Mathematics and Statistics is one of Australia’s premier mathematics communities, renowned for excellence in research, teaching and engagement. Home to a large, diverse cohort of academics, researchers and students, the School sustains a vibrant culture of seminars, collaborations and international partnerships. Within this, the Melbourne Applied Mathematics and Mathematical Biology Group brings together experts in fluid dynamics, mathematical biology and computation to tackle complex biological and physical systems. Supported by strong ARC funding and research infrastructure, the group offers an intellectually rich, collegial environment with deep connections across science and industry.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Douglas Brumley (details in PD) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 20 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:     JR-018985 Research Fellow in Microbial Fluid Dynamics_PD.pdf JR-018985 Research Fellow in Microbial Fluid Dynamics_PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Fellow-in-Circular-Economy_JR-019034": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Circular Economy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time (1.0 FTE); Fixed term for up to 2 years Faculty: Engineering and Information Technology School: Chemical and Biomedical Engineering Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. (pro rata) plus 17% super  Level B: AUD $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. (pro rata) plus 17% super  Shape interdisciplinary circular economy research / collaborate with national and global partners / drive real-world sustainability impact Apply now to grow your research career while advancing circular economy practice and policy. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will work closely with the Chair of Circular Economy to design and deliver innovative research programmes. You will build partnerships across disciplines, industry and government to translate insights into measurable sustainability outcomes. What you will deliver:   Design and deliver high-quality circular economy research projects in interdisciplinary teams with industry, government and campus partners. Identify and secure new collaborative funding opportunities, including proposals in plastics, metals, energy and related systems. Develop and manage project plans, budgets, timelines and milestones to ensure successful delivery of research programmes. Coordinate engagement activities including workshops, seminars and events that strengthen partnerships and visibility for circular economy. Contribute to advancing sustainability and circular economy at the University through publications, presentations and internal initiatives. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in a relevant field such as sustainability, circular economy or engineering. Demonstrate experience developing collaborative funding opportunities with partners in industry, government or research organisations. Work effectively within interdisciplinary research teams, building constructive relationships across diverse disciplines and stakeholder groups. Manage projects and budgets confidently, setting priorities, monitoring progress and delivering agreed outcomes on time. Communicate complex ideas clearly, with strong written, oral and workshop facilitation skills across in-person and online formats. Bring an outstanding track record in circular economy or related project areas, including independent, self-directed research (quantitative and/or qualitative). For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation. Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being.  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning. Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company.  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program.  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team Within the School of Chemical and Biomedical Engineering, you will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary research community. You will collaborate closely with the Department of Chemical Engineering, renowned for world-leading industry-engaged sustainability research. You will benefit from FEIT’s strong partnerships, mission-led 2035 strategy and major circular economy research initiatives and centres. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Brett Verity at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Position Description:   JR-019034 Research Fellow in Circular Economy PD.doc JR-019034 Research Fellow in Circular Economy PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Senior-Project-Officer_JR-018922": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time; Fixed term for 12 months   Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department/School: Department of Microbiology and Immunology, School of Biomedical Sciences  Salary: $111,275 – $120,435 p.a. plus 17% super   Impact global pathogen surveillance / coordinate platform deployments / collaborate with diverse public health partners   Apply now to lead Trakka implementations and help transform pathogen genomics into actionable public health insight.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.    The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    You will coordinate platform deployments that enable real time pathogen genomics for critical public health decisions.  You will partner with technical and public health teams, shaping how genomic data informs surveillance, research and biosecurity.  You will gain experience working within a leading infection and immunity institute on nationally significant digital infrastructure. What you will deliver:     Lead planning and coordination of platform deployments across various health settings.   Develop and manage implementation plans, timelines, milestones and resources to support effective platform adoption.   Coordinate stakeholder engagement, onboarding, training and user acceptance testing to ensure deployment readiness.   Monitor project progress, manage risks and issues, and report outcomes, benefits and key performance indicators.   Liaise between technical teams and end users, capturing feedback and supporting continuous platform improvement.   You may be a great fit if you:     Hold a tertiary qualification in IT, data science, public health or a closely related discipline.   Bring demonstrated experience managing complex projects, products or implementations involving IT systems or data platforms.   Demonstrate strong stakeholder engagement skills and success building relationships across diverse partner organisations.   Have experience working with information systems, digital platforms or data driven public health programmes.   Apply excellent organisational, communication and problem solving capabilities within fast paced, multidisciplinary environments.   Offer experience or strong interest in public health, genomics, bioinformatics or health data management contexts.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.    What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team   You will join the Department of Microbiology and Immunology within the School of Biomedical Sciences. Your role sits jointly in the Centre for Pathogen Genomics and the Microbiological Diagnostic Unit Public Health Laboratory. Together, these teams deliver integrated pathogen genomics, diagnostics and surveillance to support public health decision making. You will collaborate with leading experts across genomics, bioinformatics, epidemiology, laboratory science and capacity building. You will be part of an inclusive, values driven environment focused on innovation, impact and world health outcomes.     Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .    Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.    You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Tuyet Hoang at tuyet.hoang@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: Sunday 8 November 1:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:     JR-018922 Senior Project Officer - PD.docx   JR-018922 Senior Project Officer - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Lecturer-in-Social-Work--Field-Education-_JR-018793-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Social Work (Field Education)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nRole type:  1 x Full Time, Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences School:  School of Health Sciences Salary: Level B - $124,656 – $148,023 per annum p.a. plus 17% super  Deliver outstanding social work field education / coordinate transformative MSW placements / create rich practice-based learning with diverse agencies Apply now to shape students’ professional growth, building confident practitioners ready for evolving community needs. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will coordinate and enhance MSW field education, creating safe, high-quality and transformative practice learning experiences. You will work closely with students, educators and placement agencies to match learning opportunities to students’ goals. You will contribute to teaching, innovation and scholarship that strengthen social work education and professional practice. What you will deliver:   Develop and coordinate diverse social work placements that provide high-quality, safe and inclusive practice learning experiences. Facilitate student learning through supervision, liaison and tutorial visits, supporting students and placement educators in practice settings. Contribute to assessment and progression by undertaking placement visits, evaluating capabilities and addressing emerging learning needs. Support students experiencing placement challenges, negotiating solutions with agencies, supervisors and subject coordinators where required. Collaborate on field education subject delivery, integration seminars and innovations connecting academic learning with professional practice.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD or advanced social work practice profile and eligibility for Australian Association of Social Workers membership. Bring at least five years’ post-qualification social work experience, ideally including supervisory, placement or educational responsibilities. Demonstrate excellence in teaching and student engagement, with commitment to innovative, continuously improving practice education approaches. Communicate clearly and collaboratively, building strong relationships with students, agencies, field educators and academic colleagues. Apply well-developed negotiation, conflict resolution and issue management skills in complex or sensitive placement situations. Contribute to research or scholarship in field education, professional learning or social work practice development. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team - You will join the Department of Social Work within the School of Health Sciences at the University of Melbourne. Our dynamic academic community is committed to social justice, evidence-informed practice and outstanding professional education. We partner with diverse health, community and human service organisations to deliver impactful placements and learning experiences. You will be supported by collegial peers and leaders who value collaboration, innovation and student-centred practice. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Milena Heinsch For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 29 October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018793 Lecturer in Social Work (Field Education) PD.docx JR-018793 Lecturer in Social Work (Field Education) PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Aquarium-Technician_JR-019108-1": {
      "title": "Aquarium Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing  Department: ESG – Melbourne Bioresources - COO Portfolio  Salary: UOM 3 - $69,554 - $76,795 p.a. plus 17% super  Work in a state-of-the-art zebrafish research facility / support world-class scientific discovery / develop specialist aquaculture and animal care skills  Join a dedicated animal care team and apply your aquaculture skills to support impactful research.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   In this Aquarium Technician role, you will ensure the health and welfare of zebrafish that underpin cutting-edge research. You will develop specialised aquaculture and laboratory animal skills while working in a highly collaborative, research-focused environment.  What you will deliver:   Provide daily husbandry, feeding, cleaning and health monitoring of zebrafish to the highest animal welfare standards.  Perform routine tank cleaning, facility hygiene tasks and basic equipment care to ensure reliable aquarium operations.  Conduct regular water quality testing, record results accurately and report issues promptly to support stable system performance.  Maintain breeding colonies and live food cultures to support research requirements and colony health.  Adhere to regulatory and ethical requirements, including AWV, POCTA, OGTR and DAFF, in all facility activities.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold completed Year 12 and a Certificate II in aquaculture, or equivalent relevant experience and training.  Demonstrate strong attention to detail, clear communication skills and reliable record-keeping in technical or animal care settings.  Apply sound organisational skills to manage routine tasks efficiently and follow established standard operating procedures independently.  Collaborate effectively within a small technical team to support consistent facility operations and animal welfare outcomes.  Bring experience with recirculating aquaculture systems, equipment maintenance or aquatic animal health monitoring, or similar environments.  Offer prior experience working in a laboratory animal facility or regulated animal research environment.  Are available to undertake occasional weekend work as required to support facility operations.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team:   Within the Chief Operating Officer Portfolio, you will join the Melbourne Bioresources platform in the state-of-the-art Zebrafish Aquarium. The team enables high-impact research by providing exceptional animal care, technical support and reliable aquatic systems for University of Melbourne researchers. You will be part of a collaborative, detail-focused group that values animal welfare, continuous improvement and developing specialist skills in aquaculture and laboratory animal science.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application.   Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​ Sunday 25 October 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:   PD_JR-019108 Aquarium Technician .docx  PD_JR-019108 Aquarium Technician .pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Assistant-to-Head-of-Department_JR-019137": {
      "title": "Research Assistant to Head of Department",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time/ Full Time; 0.5 – 1 FTE; Fixed-term for 5 months Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/ School: Department of Medical Education, Melbourne Medical School Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. pro rata for part time plus 17% super Work alongside a senior Head of Department / deepen qualitative and quantitative research expertise / contribute to medical education impact Apply your research skills in medical education and grow your academic profile in a supportive team Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will work closely with the Head of Department on high-quality medical education research projects. You will strengthen your qualitative and quantitative analysis skills while contributing to publications, presentations and curriculum innovation. What you will deliver:   Provide research and administrative support to the Head of Department across multiple medical education projects. Conduct qualitative analyses and support quantitative analyses of administrative, longitudinal and linked assessment datasets. Manage, assemble and clean datasets, ensuring data quality for reporting, manuscripts and conference presentations. Coordinate ethics applications and processes, and maintain productive relationships with project partners and stakeholders. Conduct literature searches and systematic reviews, and contribute to written reports, papers and presentations.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold an undergraduate degree in a relevant discipline and an interest in medical education research. Use qualitative analysis software such as NVivo and manage survey data using platforms like Qualtrics. Demonstrate strong skills in literature review, synthesis of peer-reviewed and grey literature, and academic writing. Understand human research ethics principles and have experience preparing and managing ethics submissions. Bring excellent attention to detail, diligence and organisational skills to manage concurrent research tasks and deadlines. Value collaborative work in multidisciplinary teams and communicate effectively with diverse colleagues and stakeholders. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – You will join the Department of Medical Education within the Melbourne Medical School at Parkville. The Department leads the Doctor of Medicine curriculum, postgraduate programmes and research that improves health professional education. Research spans work integrated learning, curriculum and assessment, healthcare communication and student engagement. You will collaborate with academics, clinicians and professional staff in a supportive, values-driven environment encouraging innovation. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Anna Ryan by emailing annatr@unimelb.edu.au. For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 29 October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019137 Research Assistant to Head of Department PD.docx JR-019137 Research Assistant to Head of Department PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Assistant_JR-018720": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Sleep and Daily Rhythms",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (1 year) Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department: Centre for Youth Mental Health Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super Work in a world-leading youth mental health centre / drive NHMRC-funded LUMOS sleep–circadian research / shape early psychosis detection and care Apply now to support young people at risk of psychosis through rigorous sleep and circadian research Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne  We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will support the LUMOS study, tracking sleep and circadian rhythms in youth psychosis. You will gain hands-on experience in clinical interviewing, neurocognitive assessment and high-quality longitudinal research. You will work within a multidisciplinary team at Orygen, translating discoveries into better early intervention decisions. Occasional off-site (e.g. at Orygen headspace centres) visits to participants, regular attendance at the study sites, and some out of hours work will be required. What you will deliver: Recruit and enrol young participants into the LUMOS sleep and circadian study across clinical services. Conduct structured clinical interviews and neurocognitive assessments, including pupil measures and computerised cognitive tasks. Maintain accurate, confidential, audit-ready participant records and study databases in line with Good Clinical Practice guidelines. Liaise with clinical teams, young people and families to support recruitment, follow-up and engagement over 12 months. Assist with ethics and governance submissions, standard operating procedures, manuscripts and presentations for multidisciplinary project meetings. You may be a great fit if you: Hold an honours-level degree in behavioural or social sciences, or equivalent education and relevant experience. Demonstrate strong interest in youth mental health and sound understanding of psychiatric diagnoses and disorders. Conduct structured interviews and engage sensitively with young people experiencing mental health difficulties. Follow complex research protocols, maintain precise records and troubleshoot issues in fast-paced clinical research settings. Use electronic data capture platforms, preferably REDCap, for secure entry, verification and basic data analysis. Manage workloads independently across multiple sites, collaborating with multidisciplinary stakeholders and meeting competing deadlines. For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – Centre for Youth Mental Health At the Centre for Youth Mental Health, you will join a pioneering leader in youth mental health research. Orygen’s multidisciplinary teams integrate clinical services, research and knowledge translation to improve outcomes for young people. You will work alongside clinicians, scientists and allied professionals within Australia’s largest health sciences faculty. The team values curiosity, rigour, collaboration and genuine impact on early intervention and prevention in psychosis. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process . Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Dr Mag de Rozario at mag.derozario@unimelb.edu.au . For questions related to our recruitment process email Vanessa Phan at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 29 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: PD_JR-018720 Research Assistant in Sleep and Daily Rhythms.docx PD_JR-018720 Research Assistant in Sleep and Daily Rhythms.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/The-Director--Enterprise-Partnerships--Health-and-Life-Sciences-_JR-019078": {
      "title": "Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health and Life Sciences)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nDirector, Enterprise Partnerships (Health and Life Sciences) Melbourne Enterprise | Melbourne Research and Enterprise Portfolio Full-time | Fixed term for 5 years The University of Melbourne is home to some of Australia's most significant research, innovation and entrepreneurial capabilities, with a track record that includes strategic agreements with leading global organisations. This is a significant opportunity to build on that foundation, grow the University's external partnerships and unlock new opportunities across the health and life sciences sector. We are seeking an exceptional senior leader to lead Enterprise Partnerships across health and life sciences. Reporting to the Executive Director, Partnerships and Precincts, you will identify, shape and convert strategic opportunities that connect the University's world-class capabilities with industry, government, health services, medical research institutes and other key partners. As a senior institutional interface, you will bring together expertise from across the University to develop high-impact partnerships that accelerate research translation, support improved health outcomes, build workforce capability, influence policy and generate lasting economic and societal impact. The opportunity This is a career-defining opportunity to build on a strong foundation of global partnerships and shape the next generation of health and life sciences engagement at one of the world's leading universities. Building on a strong track record of strategic agreements that are among the first of their kind in Australia, you will have the opportunity to develop new partnerships and extend the University's impact across Australia and internationally. You will lead strategic partnership development, business development and key account management across the sector, building trusted relationships with senior external stakeholders and translating the University's research, education, talent and innovation capabilities into compelling partner propositions. You will also strengthen a more market-facing, sector-led and partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, while leading and developing a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. Key responsibilities include: Originate, shape and convert high-value strategic partnerships, collaborations and commercial opportunities across the health and life sciences sector. Build influential relationships with senior leaders across industry, government, healthcare, medical research and innovation, both nationally and internationally. Translate the University's research, education, talent, commercialisation and enterprise capabilities into compelling value propositions for partners. Identify and prioritise a strong pipeline of opportunities, applying commercial judgement, governance and disciplined account and performance management. Mobilise multidisciplinary expertise across faculties, research institutes and professional services to respond to complex opportunities and maximise impact. Strengthen a sector-led, partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, including data-informed decision-making and effective use of CRM and emerging technologies. Lead and develop a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. About you You are an accomplished partnerships, commercial, business development or enterprise leader with deep knowledge of the health and life sciences sector and a strong track record of creating and growing complex, high-value partnerships. You bring strategic insight, commercial acumen and exceptional relationship-building capability, with the credibility to engage senior stakeholders, navigate complex environments and turn opportunities into meaningful outcomes. You will bring: Significant experience leading strategic partnerships, business development or commercial activity within industry, government, healthcare, research or innovation environments. Deep knowledge of the health and life sciences sector, with the ability to identify emerging trends across biotechnology, medical technologies, pharmaceuticals, precision medicine, research translation and related fields and translate these into strategic partnership opportunities. Experience working within, or closely alongside, research-intensive, innovation-led or knowledge-based organisations, with the ability to connect complex research capability with external partner needs. A strong track record of establishing and growing influential relationships across industry, government, healthcare, research and innovation ecosystems and converting these into strategic opportunities. Demonstrated commercial judgement in structuring complex partnerships, evaluating opportunities, managing risk and navigating governance to achieve sustainable outcomes. Experience shaping new approaches, capabilities or operating models in complex environments and bringing diverse stakeholders with you. Outstanding communication, influencing and stakeholder engagement skills, with the judgement and credibility to build alignment and influence senior decision-makers. A collaborative and inclusive leadership style, with experience setting direction, building capability and developing high-performing teams. How to apply To apply, please submit: A current CV. A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your suitability for the role. For role or application process enquiries, please contact Lucy Porter , Executive Recruitment Partner, at exec-recruitment@unimelb.edu.au . If you are ready to build on world-class research and innovation capabilities, grow high-impact partnerships and shape lasting health, economic and societal impact, we invite you to apply. Applications must be submitted through the online application portal and will not be accepted by email. Please note: The University is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Applications close: Monday 9th November 2026 at 11:55pm Melbourne time.   Position Description: PD - Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health, LS).pdf PD - Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health, LS).docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Director--Partnership-Success-Group_JR-019079": {
      "title": "Director, Partnership Success Group",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nDirector, Partnership Success Group Melbourne Enterprise | Melbourne Research and Enterprise Portfolio Full-time | Fixed term for 5 years The University of Melbourne is one of the world's leading research-intensive universities, with the research capability, talent and enterprise ambition to solve society's most significant challenges. Melbourne Enterprise is building a more coordinated, market-facing and partner-centric institution, connecting world-class research and expertise with industry, government and other external partners. We are seeking an exceptional senior leader to lead the Partnership Success Group, a pivotal enterprise capability that will shape how the University mobilises, delivers and deepens partnerships across research, innovation and workforce development. Reporting to the Executive Director, Partnerships and Precincts, you will bring together commercial development, market intelligence and systems, partnership mobilisation and delivery, workforce capability and organisational capability development to enable partnership growth at scale. As a key institutional leader, you will work across faculties, Melbourne Enterprise and professional services to mobilise expertise and resources around complex opportunities, strengthen commercial and delivery capability, and create a consistently exceptional partner experience. You will play a central role in establishing the systems, frameworks, governance and ways of working that enable a consistent, market-facing and partner-centric approach to partnering across the University. The opportunity This is a career-defining opportunity to build enterprise capability that will shape how one of the world's leading universities develops, mobilises and delivers partnerships at scale. You will lead the Partnership Success Group, strengthening the University's ability to shape opportunities, structure partnerships and deliver them effectively. Working closely with sector-aligned Enterprise Partnerships leaders and colleagues across the University, you will bring together commercial, delivery and institutional capability to turn major opportunities into enduring partnerships and measurable impact. You will also lead the University's workforce capability partnering function, bringing together professional education, custom education and industry talent solutions into a coordinated enterprise offering that connects partners with the University's education, workforce and talent capabilities, including students, PhDs and graduates. A key focus will be building the systems, intelligence and organisational capability that enable better decision-making, effective prioritisation, strong partnership performance and an exceptional experience for partners. Key responsibilities include: Establish and lead the Partnership Success Group, building the commercial, delivery and organisational capability required to mobilise and deliver partnerships at scale. Lead the University's workforce capability partnering function, aligning professional education, custom education and industry talent solutions with partner needs and institutional priorities. Provide strategic leadership for partnership mobilisation and delivery, ensuring major opportunities and partnerships are governed effectively, executed consistently and deliver value for partners and the University. Lead the uplift of partnership development capability across research, innovation and workforce development, including opportunity shaping, business case development, partnership structuring and commercial frameworks. Establish and enhance partner intelligence, CRM, pipeline management and reporting capabilities to support effective prioritisation, resource allocation and decision-making. Mobilise cross-functional teams and institutional resources around complex opportunities, working across faculties and professional services to maximise partnership outcomes. Establish best-practice frameworks, standards and tools that embed consistent ways of working and build enterprise-wide partnership capability. About you You are an accomplished partnerships, commercial, business development or enterprise transformation leader with a strong track record of building capability, leading complex functions and converting opportunities into sustainable organisational, commercial or societal outcomes. You bring strategic insight, commercial acumen and exceptional stakeholder engagement capability, with the credibility to work across a complex, matrixed organisation and influence senior executives, academic leaders, professional teams and external partners. You will bring: Significant experience leading strategic growth, business development, partnership delivery or enterprise transformation functions, with a track record of converting opportunities into sustainable outcomes. Demonstrated ability to mobilise diverse functions around large-scale, complex and high-value opportunities, bringing together commercial, delivery, technical and stakeholder capability to achieve institutional outcomes. Strong technology-enabled leadership, championing digital innovation, human-centred design and data-driven approaches to partner engagement, pipeline management and growth. An ability to create the conditions and environment for innovation. Strong commercial acumen, including experience developing business cases, shaping partnership models, structuring opportunities and navigating financial, governance, risk and delivery considerations. Proven experience establishing, leading or transforming complex functions, operating models or organisational capabilities, including embedding new ways of working and leading organisational change. Exceptional stakeholder engagement, influencing and negotiation skills, with the ability to build credibility and trust with senior executives, academic leaders, professional teams and external partners. Highly developed strategic judgement and problem-solving skills, with the agility to operate in ambiguity, balance competing priorities and make sound decisions at pace. A collaborative and influential leadership style, with the ability to break down organisational silos, align diverse stakeholders around shared outcomes and build trusted relationships across the University. How to apply To apply, please submit: A current CV. A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your suitability for the role. For role or application process enquiries, please contact Lucy Porter , Executive Recruitment Partner, at exec-recruitment@unimelb.edu.au . If you are ready to build enterprise capability that will shape how the University develops, delivers and deepens partnerships at scale, we invite you to apply. Applications must be submitted through the online application portal and will not be accepted by email. Please note: The University is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Applications close: Monday 2nd November 2026 at 11:55pm Melbourne time. Position Description: PD - JR-019079 Director, Partnership Success Group.pdf PD - JR-019079 Director, Partnership Success Group.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Deputy-Director-of-Indigenous-Medical-Education_JR-018456-1": {
      "title": "Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full Time; Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department: Department of Medical Education Salary: Level B $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Shape Indigenous medical education across Melbourne’s Doctor of Medicine program through curriculum innovation, meaningful partnerships and culturally safe practice to improve First Nations health outcomes. Apply now to drive strategic curriculum leadership, evidence-informed teaching and meaningful community engagement in one of Australia’s leading medical schools. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as these positions are exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic).  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   Help shape the next generation of doctors through leadership in Indigenous medical education. You will guide curriculum innovation, assessment and community engagement initiatives that advance culturally safe practice and contribute to improved First Nations health outcomes. What you will deliver:   Provide leadership for the development and delivery of First Nations health education across the Doctor of Medicine program, ensuring alignment with learning outcomes, curriculum priorities and accreditation requirements. Drive the design and continuous improvement of culturally safe, evidence-informed learning and assessment practices, including innovative teaching resources, placements and digital learning experiences. Support and enable high-performing teams and partnerships, fostering collaboration across academic, clinical and community settings to achieve impactful educational outcomes. Strengthen engagement with First Nations communities and health organisations, ensuring Indigenous knowledges, perspectives and priorities inform curriculum, educational practice and quality improvement activities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a tertiary qualification in health, education, public health or an equivalent combination of training. Bring experience in health professional education, with expertise in curriculum design, assessment and educational quality improvement in a tertiary or healthcare setting. Demonstrate leadership and collaboration skills, with the ability to influence practice, support teams and work effectively across complex organisational environments. Build strong and respectful relationships with diverse stakeholders, including First Nations communities, health services, academic colleagues and partner organisations. Have a strong understanding of First Nations health, cultural safety and health equity, together with a commitment to improving outcomes for First Nations peoples through education. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – “The Wurru Wurru Health Unit”   At the Wurru Wurru Health Unit, we are shaping the future of medical education. Through partnerships with First Nations communities, clinicians and educators we lead the design and delivery of First Nations health education that prepares future doctors to provide culturally safe, responsive and equitable care. As part of our team, you will have the opportunity to influence one of Australia's leading medical programs, contribute to sector-leading curriculum innovation, and work alongside passionate colleagues dedicated to advancing First Nations health and educational excellence. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the our recruitment process, including application tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A valid Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please submit: A current resume (CV) A cover letter outlining your interest in the role and relevant experience Your responses to the selection criteria, found in the position description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Dr Bridie Hofstee at b.hofstee@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process, contact Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments, contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 8 November 2026 11:55 PM; AEDT.  Position Description:   JR-018456 Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education PD.docx JR-018456 Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Director--Enterprise-Partnerships--Energy--Primary-Industries---Education-_JR-019080": {
      "title": "Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries & Education)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nDirector, Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries & Education) Melbourne Enterprise | Melbourne Research and Enterprise Portfolio Continuing | Fixed-term for 5 years The University of Melbourne is home to some of Australia's most significant research, innovation and entrepreneurial capabilities, with a track record that includes strategic agreements with leading global organisations. This is a significant opportunity to build on that foundation, grow the University's external partnerships and unlock new opportunities across energy, primary industries and education. We are seeking an exceptional senior leader to lead Enterprise Partnerships across energy, primary industries and education. Reporting to the Executive Director, Partnerships and Precincts, you will identify, shape and convert strategic opportunities that connect the University's world-class capabilities with industry, government, primary industries, education providers and other key partners. As a senior institutional interface, you will bring together expertise from across the University to develop high-impact partnerships that accelerate the energy transition, strengthen sustainable and productive primary industries, build workforce capability, influence policy and generate lasting economic and societal impact. The opportunity This is a career-defining opportunity to build on a strong foundation of global partnerships and shape the next generation of engagement across energy, primary industries and education at one of the world's leading universities. Building on a strong track record of strategic agreements that are among the first of their kind in Australia, you will have the opportunity to develop new partnerships and extend the University's impact across Australia and internationally. You will lead strategic partnership development, business development and key account management across the sectors, building trusted relationships with senior external stakeholders and translating the University's research, education, talent and innovation capabilities into compelling partner propositions. You will also strengthen a more market-facing, sector-led and partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, while leading and developing a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. Key responsibilities include: Originate, shape and convert high-value strategic partnerships, collaborations and commercial opportunities across energy, primary industries and education. Build influential relationships with senior leaders across industry, government, research, education, primary industries and innovation, both nationally and internationally. Translate the University's research, education, talent, commercialisation and enterprise capabilities into compelling value propositions for partners. Identify and prioritise a strong pipeline of opportunities, applying commercial judgement, governance and disciplined account and performance management. Mobilise multidisciplinary expertise across faculties, research institutes and professional services to respond to complex opportunities and maximise impact. Strengthen a sector-led, partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, including data-informed decision-making and effective use of CRM and emerging technologies. Lead and develop a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. About you You are an accomplished partnerships, commercial, business development or enterprise leader with deep knowledge of the energy, primary industries and/or education sectors and a strong track record of creating and growing complex, high-value partnerships. You bring strategic insight, commercial acumen and exceptional relationship-building capability, with the credibility to engage senior stakeholders, navigate complex environments and turn opportunities into meaningful outcomes. You will bring: Significant experience leading strategic partnerships, business development or commercial activity within industry, government, research, education, primary industries or innovation environments. Deep knowledge of the energy, primary industries and/or education landscape, with the ability to identify emerging trends across energy transition, climate resilience, biodiversity and environmental sustainability, sustainable agriculture and food systems, workforce development, educational innovation and lifelong learning, and translate these into strategic partnership opportunities. Experience working within, or closely alongside, research-intensive, innovation-led or knowledge-based organisations, with the ability to connect complex research capability with external partner needs. A strong track record of establishing and growing influential relationships across industry, government, research, education, sustainability and innovation ecosystems and converting these into strategic opportunities. Demonstrated commercial judgement in structuring complex partnerships, evaluating opportunities, managing risk and navigating governance to achieve sustainable outcomes. Experience shaping new approaches, capabilities or operating models in complex environments and bringing diverse stakeholders with you. Outstanding communication, influencing and stakeholder engagement skills, with the judgement and credibility to build alignment and influence senior decision-makers. A collaborative and inclusive leadership style, with experience setting direction, building capability and developing high-performing teams. How to apply To apply, please submit: A current CV. A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your suitability for the role. For role or application process enquiries, please contact Lucy Porter, Executive Recruitment Partner, at exec-recruitment@unimelb.edu.au . If you are ready to build on world-class research and innovation capabilities, grow high-impact partnerships and shape lasting environmental, economic, workforce and societal impact, we invite you to apply. Applications must be submitted through the online application portal and will not be accepted by email. Please note: The University is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Applications close: Monday 2nd November at 11:55pm Melbourne time. Position Description: PD - JR-019080 Director Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries, Education).pdf PD - JR-019080 Director Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries, Education).docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Brisbane-Queensland/Manager---Ecosystem-Partnerships---Engagement--QLD--NSW---ACT-_JR-018830": {
      "title": "Manager – Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (QLD, NSW & ACT)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part Time (0.6FTE); Fixed Term for 12 months Faculty: Faculty of Business & Economics Location : Brisbane, Queensland Department:  Dilin Duwa Centre for Indigenous Business Leadership Salary: $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. pro-rata for part time plus 17% super  Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic). Strategic Indigenous ecosystem leadership across QLD, NSW and ACT / community and partner engagement / grow impact through funded initiative Join Dilin Duwa to strengthen Indigenous business networks and lead high impact partnerships across QLD, NSW and ACT. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead ecosystem partnerships and engagement across eastern Australia, connecting Indigenous businesses, communities and partners. You will translate relationships into new programmes, initiatives and revenue that grow Dilin Duwa’s reach and impact. What you will deliver:   Lead regional engagement initiatives, workshops and dialogues that are culturally grounded and aligned with Dilin Duwa. Build and manage strategic partnerships across the Indigenous business ecosystem, including corporations, government, universities and philanthropy. Drive business development opportunities, developing proposals, funding submissions and enterprise services that expand contracted revenue. Strengthen alumni, leadership and regional networks of Indigenous entrepreneurs and organisations connected to Dilin Duwa. Oversee programme delivery and partnerships from planning through evaluation, monitoring outcomes and reporting insights for improvement. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a relevant postgraduate qualification or equivalent senior experience in a related field. Be of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent and bring lived experience to this work. Demonstrate strong experience working with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities or organisations. Build and sustain complex relationships and partnerships across community, corporate, government and ecosystem stakeholders. Manage programs and projects end-to-end, with strong organisational skills and high-level project management capability. Use Microsoft Office confidently and adapt quickly to new software, systems and digital engagement tools. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team Dilin Duwa is a pioneering Centre for Indigenous Business Leadership within the Faculty of Business and Economics. The Centre partners with Melbourne Business School to deliver research, education and ecosystem services nationally. You will join a collaborative team committed to Indigenous economic independence and thriving Indigenous enterprises. The team values culturally grounded practice, deep community engagement and evidence based impact. Based in Brisbane, you will connect regional ecosystems with Dilin Duwa’s national networks and capabilities.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Paul Paton by emailing p.paton@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Peterson at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Tuesday 27 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   Word : Manager - Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (QLD, NSW & ACT)_PD (1).docx PDF : Manager - Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (QLD, NSW & ACT)_PD (1).pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Perth-Western-Australia/Manager---Ecosystem-Partnerships---Engagement--WA--SA---NT-_JR-018829-1": {
      "title": "Manager – Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (WA, SA & NT)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.6 FTE); Fixed-term, 12 months   Faculty: Business and Economics Location: Perth, Western Australia Department/School: Dilin Duwa Centre for Indigenous Business Leadership Salary: AUD $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% super  Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic). Strategic Indigenous ecosystem leadership across WA, SA and NT / community and partner engagement / grow impact through funded initiative Join Dilin Duwa to strengthen Indigenous business networks and lead high impact partnerships across WA, SA and NT. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead ecosystem partnerships and engagement for Dilin Duwa across WA, SA and NT. You will build culturally grounded relationships with Indigenous communities and partners, shaping programs that grow economic independence. You will translate networks into new initiatives, collaborations and revenue streams that expand the Centre’s national impact. What you will deliver:   Lead culturally grounded engagement initiatives, regional workshops and leadership programs with Indigenous communities and partners across the region. Develop and manage strategic partnerships with Indigenous business ecosystem organisations, government, corporates, universities and philanthropic stakeholders. Drive business development by identifying enterprise service opportunities, preparing proposals and funding submissions, and growing contracted revenue. Expand Dilin Duwa’s alumni and leadership networks, strengthening national connections among Indigenous entrepreneurs and organisations. Oversee project delivery, contractors and facilitators, monitoring outcomes and reporting on program impact, risks and improvements.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a relevant postgraduate qualification or bring equivalent senior experience in a related field. Be of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent and bring lived experience to this work. Demonstrate strong experience working with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities or organisations on programs or initiatives. Bring proven capability in relationship building, partnership development, stakeholder engagement and business development across diverse sectors. Manage programs or projects with high level organisational skills, reporting on outcomes, risks and impact. Use Microsoft Office confidently and quickly learn new software to support program, partnership and reporting activities. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team Dilin Duwa is a pioneering Centre for Indigenous Business Leadership within the Faculty of Business and Economics. The Centre partners with Melbourne Business School to deliver research, education and ecosystem services nationally. You will join a collaborative team committed to Indigenous economic independence and thriving Indigenous enterprises. The team values culturally grounded practice, deep community engagement and evidence based impact. Based in Perth, you will connect regional ecosystems with Dilin Duwa’s national networks and capabilities.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Paul Paton at p.paton@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Tuesday 27 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: Word : Manager - Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (WA, SA & NT)_PD (1).docx PDF : Manager - Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (WA, SA & NT)_PD (1).pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Graduate-Researcher-Academic-Associate-in-History-and-Theory_JR-019174": {
      "title": "Graduate Researcher Academic Associate in History and Theory",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nJob no: several new positions Location:  Parkville Role type:  0.2 FTE Part-time Fixed-term for 24-months, tied to candidature and valid work rights Faculty:  Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning Salary:  Level A.2 – $92,243 per annum, pro rata for part-time, plus 17% super   New opportunity to gain meaningful academic experience in education in the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning Undertake career advancing professional development, including 30 hours paid professional development Salary packaging, subsidised health and wellbeing services, fitness and cultural clubs, Myki discounts, and a 25% discount on graduate courses to our staff and their immediate families   About the Role The Faculty invites applications for several new Graduate Researcher Academic Associate (GRAA) positions from graduate researchers currently enrolled in a PhD in the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning with discipline and domain knowledge in the subjects comprising the Undergraduate Bachelor of Design with a focus in built environment history and theory taught in the Architecture Major.    As a GRAA in History and Theory you will contribute positively, both individually and as part of a team, to educating, inspiring and supporting students. The GRAAs will be responsible for carrying out education related activity for one or more subjects. Key education responsibilities will include: ·         Teaching, including through tutorials, seminars, workshops ·         Undertaking preparation and associated work, including teaching and student consultation ·         Regular consultation with students, monitor discussion boards/forums, respond to queries, conduct exam revision sessions ·         Marking a share of project assessment submissions ·         Managing student submissions (e.g. chasing up missing submissions, handling late submissions, handling remark requests, assessment feedback)]   In addition, you will be supported to participate as an academic member of the Faculty in contributing to the Faculty’s academic mission, life and community and supported to engage in at least 30 hours of paid professional development.   Who We Are Looking For We are seeking applicants enrolled as a University of Melbourne student undertaking a PhD in the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning with discipline knowledge in built environment history and theory You will be a passionate educator and have the ability to contribute positively, both individually and as part of a team, to educating, inspiring and supporting students. You will also have excellent written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills and excellent organisational skills with the ability to manage one’s own time effectively, administer multiple tasks simultaneously and meet deadlines. For further information regarding responsibilities and requirements, please refer to the attached PD. Position Description JR-019174 Graduate Research Academic Associate in History and Theory PD.docx JR-019174 Graduate Research Academic Associate in History and Theory PD.pdf This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check. This is essential to guarantee a secure environment for all individuals at the University. In the case the selected candidate doesn't have a valid Working with Children Check, they will be able to apply for the WWCC as part of the initial onboarding procedures."
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Academic-Tutor---Murrup-Barak_JR-018018": {
      "title": "Academic Tutor - Murrup Barak",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nRole type:   Part-time (minimum 0.2 FTE); Fixed term  Department/ School:   Indigenous Portfolio – Murrup Barak  Salary: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. (pro rata for part-time) plus 17% super   Make a direct impact on Indigenous student success / share real-world discipline expertise / join a supportive Indigenous-led team  Apply now to use your discipline expertise to empower Indigenous students through tailored, high-impact tutoring.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    You will provide focused academic tutoring that strengthens success and confidence for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students.  You will join a collaborative team of tutors, shaping culturally safe, empowering learning experiences across diverse disciplines.  What you will deliver:    Design and deliver weekly discipline-specific tutoring sessions, individually and in groups, to support student learning goals.  Guide students to develop self-directed learning strategies while maintaining strong academic standards and integrity.  Collaborate with fellow tutors to create, refine and share discipline resources and participate in professional development.  Build culturally safe, supportive learning environments and make referrals to the Student Success Team when needed.  Maintain accurate tutoring records, schedules and progress notes, ensuring program systems are used effectively.    You may be a great fit if you:    Hold an undergraduate degree and strong knowledge of your discipline at postgraduate level.  Bring experience supporting or understanding Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students in tertiary learning environments.  Demonstrate awareness of systemic barriers affecting Indigenous students and commitment to culturally safe practice.  Coach learners in complex concepts, adapting explanations for different levels and assessment expectations.  Manage tutoring schedules reliably, using program systems and processes to track attendance and progress.  Collaborate effectively within a tutor team and with Student Success colleagues to support student wellbeing.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team   You will join Murrup Barak, the Indigenous student services hub within the University’s Indigenous Portfolio.  The team collaborates across the University to provide safe, empowering support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students.  You will be part of a growing tutor cohort, valued for discipline expertise, cultural respect and student-centred practice.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application.   Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Josh Cubillo at josh.cubillo@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.                                            Applications close: Tuesday 27th October11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:   JR-018018 Academic Tutor - Murrup Barak - PD.pdf   JR-018018 Academic Tutor - Murrup Barak - PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow-in-Mathematics_JR-018338": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Mathematics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed term (2 years) Faculty: Science School : School of Mathematics and Statistics Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super ARC-funded Langlands research role / strong emphasis on high-quality mathematical publications / collaborative, internationally connected research environment Apply now to drive cutting-edge research in representation theory and build a strong independent research profile. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will contribute to an ARC-funded project at the forefront of the Langlands program. You will develop your own research agenda in algebraic geometry and representation theory within a supportive team. You will gain mentoring, collaboration opportunities and a strong platform for your future academic career. What you will deliver:   Conduct internationally competitive research on representation theory and Langlands-related topics, leading to high quality journal publications. Collaborate closely with Chief Investigators to achieve ARC Discovery Project milestones and contribute to team research outputs. Present research at seminars and conferences, and engage with broader mathematical communities locally and internationally. Contribute to supervision and mentoring of postgraduate and research students in your area of expertise. Assist with preparing funding proposals, managing project administration and meeting reporting requirements for the research program. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed or submitted PhD in mathematics. Demonstrate strong expertise in algebraic geometry and representation theory. Bring experience assisting with supervision of undergraduate or higher degree research projects. Show capacity to design, manage and complete research projects with limited supervision. Communicate research clearly through high-quality written work and effective presentations in English. Value collaborative, inclusive teamwork and thrive in diverse, international mathematical environments. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of Mathematics and Statistics   The School of Mathematics and Statistics is one of Australia’s leading centres for mathematical research and teaching. It hosts major ARC centres and fellowships, creating a vibrant hub for advanced work in algebraic geometry and representation theory. Within the Faculty of Science, the School champions curiosity, excellence, impact and integrity, supported by strong global collaborations and a diverse, inclusive community. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Ting Xue (details in position description) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 1 November, 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018338 Research Fellow in Mathematics_PD.pdf JR-018338 Research Fellow in Mathematics_PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Graduate-Researcher-Academic-Associate---School-of-Electrical--Mechanical-and-Infrastructure-Engineering_JR-019623": {
      "title": "Graduate Researcher Academic Associate - School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nRole type:   Part-time (min. FTE: 0.2 max. FTE 0.3); fixed-term 24-months, tied to candidature Faculty: Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology Department/ School: School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering Salary: Level A.2 $92,243 (pro rata for part-time) p.a. plus 17% super  New opportunity to gain meaningful academic experience in education within the Department of Infrastructure Engineering in a vibrant School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering. Undertake career advancing professional development, including minimum 30 hours paid professional development Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. About the role   The School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering invites applications for two new Graduate Researcher Academic Associate (GRAA) positions from graduate researchers currently enrolled in the Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology with discipline and domain knowledge as required in the following subject areas: Position 1 (Transport Engineering): CVEN90075 Transport Infrastructure Design CVEN90048 Transport Systems Position 2 (Geomatics): GEOM30009 Imaging the Environment GEOM90007 Information Visualisation As a GRAA, the position will contribute positively, both individually and as part of a team, to educating, inspiring and supporting students. The GRAA will work directly with the teaching team and be responsible for carrying out education related activities for the mentioned subjects. Key education responsibilities will include: Delivery of sessional teaching activities (which includes tutorial, laboratory experiments and field work) in relevant Department discipline subjects Development of teaching resources for both Undergraduate and Masters subjects Other Academic activities, including student consultations, assessment feedback, monitoring discussion board and marking Improving the Accessibility and Inclusivity of teaching materials and the Canvas Learning Management System In addition, you will be supported to participate as a valued academic member of the School in contributing to the School’s academic mission, life and community and supported to engage in at least 30 hours of paid professional development. Who we are looking for We are seeking applicants enrolled as a University of Melbourne student undertaking a graduate research degree (either Doctor of Philosophy or other research doctorate) of the Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology with discipline and domain knowledge in the following areas: Transport Engineering (and the above-mentioned subject areas) Geomatics (and the above-mentioned subject areas) You will be a passionate educator and have the ability to contribute positively, both individually and as part of a team, to educating, inspiring and supporting students. You will also have excellent written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills and excellent organisational skills with the ability to manage one’s own time effectively, administer multiple tasks simultaneously and meet deadlines. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team You will join the Department of Infrastructure Engineering within the School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering. The department combines civil, environmental and geomatics engineering, focusing on sustainable solutions to complex infrastructure challenges. You will work in a vibrant, research-active environment with strong industry partnerships and interdisciplinary collaborations. The Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology champions impactful education and supports your growth as an academic engineer. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please obtain and upload a signed support letter from your PhD supervisor. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Prof Stephan Winter at winter@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Petra Newton at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 15th November at 23:55 Melbourne Time Position Description:   JR-019623 Graduate Research Academic Associate PD.docx JR-019623 Graduate Research Academic Associate PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow--Climate-Resilience-and-Adaptation-Modelling_JR-019603-2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Climate Resilience and Adaptation Modelling",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.8 FTE); Fixed term, 21 months Faculty: Business and Economics Department/ School:  Centre for Actuarial Studies, Department of Economics Salary: Level B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Transform climate risk understanding through quantitative modelling / collaborate in a global interdisciplinary lab / publish impactful research with industry partners Apply now to deliver climate resilience models that shape adaptation finance and community protection. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead quantitative modelling of climate hazards and losses within the innovative BRACE research laboratory. You will help design, develop and price adaptation and resilience instruments for catastrophe perils. You will gain rich interdisciplinary experience, working closely with academics, industry partners and funding bodies. What you will deliver:   Collaborate across BRACE work packages to develop and apply quantitative models of climate hazards and losses. Contribute to designing, developing and pricing adaptation and resilience instruments for key catastrophe perils. Lead high-quality research outputs and support competitive grant and industry funding applications across BRACE. Co-supervise Honours, Masters and PhD students while fostering a rigorous, supportive research training environment. Coordinate BRACE laboratory operations, including website content, communications, workshops and Industry Advisory Board activities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in a relevant discipline, or have all requirements completed by the commencement date. Demonstrate high-quality research through working papers, submissions or publications in refereed international journals. Communicate complex quantitative and climate concepts clearly to technical and non-technical audiences in multiple formats. Collaborate effectively in multidisciplinary teams and build strong relationships with internal and external stakeholders. Bring knowledge or experience in resilience, adaptation, climate modelling or related catastrophe risk areas. Engage confidently with industry, government or other organisations to translate research into practical applications. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Centre for Actuarial Studies You will join the Centre for Actuarial Studies within the Department of Economics. This Centre sits in the Faculty of Business and Economics and is recognised internationally for actuarial excellence. As part of the BRACE laboratory, you will work with leading academics and industry partners on catastrophe resilience. The team values rigorous research, real-world impact and collaboration, offering strong support and development opportunities. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia that are valid for the duration of this appointment. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Prof. Benjamin Avanzi at b.avanzi@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 15 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Climate Resilience and Adaption Modelling_PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Facilities-Specialist---Kenneth-Myer-Building_JR-019558-1": {
      "title": "Facilities Specialist – Kenneth Myer Building",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing  Department: Campus Operations & Delivery, Enterprise Services Group  Salary: UOM 7 - $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super  Unique facilities leadership in a flagship research building / blend technical expertise with stakeholder-focused service / drive safety, continuity and asset performance  Apply now to shape a high-impact research environment by optimising building performance, safety and stakeholder experience.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   You will lead facilities and building services maintenance activities for the Kenneth Myer Building’s complex research environment. As the principal contact for maintenance and minor works, you will safeguard safety, compliance and research continuity. You will build strong partnerships with diverse stakeholders while driving continuous improvement in asset performance and building operations.  What you will deliver:   Ensure building structure, services and plant operate safely and reliably by monitoring BAS and coordinating maintenance activities.  Coordinate reactive and planned works across PC2 laboratories and specialist spaces to minimise disruption and protect research outcomes.  Lead stakeholder communication, managing and maintaining stakeholder relationships, balancing competing priorities, identifying operational improvements and promoting a culture of continuous improvement.  Manage contractors, budgets and invoices to deliver compliant, cost-effective maintenance and minor projects aligned with lease obligations.  Maintain accurate engineering records, asset plans and risk controls, including emergency management roles and governance reporting.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold an engineering degree or equivalent facilities management experience in complex, highly serviced building environments.  Demonstrate strong contractor supervision, performance monitoring and compliance with OHS, risk management and procurement frameworks.  Possess broad technical aptitude across mechanical, electrical, hydraulic and laboratory services, including fault diagnosis and root cause analysis.  Understand bioresources and containment facilities, regulatory requirements and associated environmental, health and safety considerations.  Use digital control systems, BAS and information management tools confidently, with excellent record keeping and report writing capability.  Manage multiple priorities, budgets and stakeholder expectations effectively, driving continuous improvement and sound financial decision-making.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team:   Campus Operations & Delivery sits within the Enterprise Services Group, supporting over 600 buildings across the University’s campuses. The team ensures safe, reliable and innovative facilities that enable exceptional teaching, research and community experiences. Within this portfolio, you will focus on the Kenneth Myer Building, a flagship, multidisciplinary research environment. You will collaborate with facilities, asset management and capital works specialists who value partnership, professionalism and continuous improvement. Together, you will shape high-performing places that reflect the University’s ambition for world-class campus stewardship.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application.   Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: Tuesday 10 November ​ 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description: PD_JR-019558 Facilities Specialist – Kenneth Myer Building.docx PD_JR-019558 Facilities Specialist – Kenneth Myer Building.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Laser-Ablation--Automation-and-Digitisation-Research-Scientist_JR-018485-1": {
      "title": "Laser Ablation, Automation and Digitisation Research Scientist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing (Research Contingent)  Faculty: Faculty of Science  Department/School: School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences  Salary: Level of appointment is subject to qualification and experience Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super (PhD entry level $110,319) Level B: $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super Advance state-of-the-art laser ablation and automated analytical geochemistry capabilities in a world-class research environment. Create new ICP-MS methods and build capacity at the hardware-software interface, linking novel research capability and capacity building to translational research outcomes and impact. Lead the development of automated and digitised analytical geochemistry capabilities, advancing research and collaboration across disciplines and fostering new R&D partnerships and initiatives. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will advance analytical geochemistry by developing laboratory, automation and digital workflows that generate new and improved research capabilities and build capacity within Melbourne Analytical Geochemistry and across the Melbourne Isotope Analytics Research Platform. Combining specialist analytical expertise with digital innovation, you will help develop new methods, improve data stewardship and accessibility, and build research infrastructure that enables interdisciplinary collaboration. In addition to driving R&D, you will also contribute to research partnerships, scholarly outputs and community engagement, helping translate advances in analytical geochemistry into broader research and societal outcomes. What you will deliver:   Develop, optimise and document laboratory, analytical and digital workflows for laser ablation and solution-based mass spectrometric analyses. Advance automation, data reduction, quality assurance, imaging and digital pipelines to generate robust, reusable and well-curated research datasets. Troubleshoot and improve analytical instrumentation and digital infrastructure, developing new protocols and research capabilities in response to evolving research needs. Collaborate with researchers and external partners across academia, government and industry, contributing to research projects, funding proposals, scholarly outputs and intellectual property development. Train and mentor students and researchers, develop standard operating procedures, and communicate research capabilities and outcomes to scientific and wider communities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in trace element and/or isotope geochemistry or a related discipline, or demonstrate equivalent expertise. Bring experience in analytical geochemistry and laboratory protocols, alongside demonstrated digital capabilities in areas such as automated QA/QC, data reduction, imaging, data curation or programming. Enjoy troubleshooting technical challenges and developing or improving analytical methods, digital workflows and research infrastructure. Work effectively both independently and collaboratively, building productive relationships across multidisciplinary research teams and with academic, industry and government partners. Bring strong communication and organisational skills, with an interest in developing an academic specialist career through research outputs, training and engagement with scientific and wider communities. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences   You will join the School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences within the vibrant Faculty of Science. The Melbourne Isotope Analytics Research Platform and its MAG facility blend cutting-edge analytical infrastructure with interdisciplinary scientific collaborations. You will work alongside experts in geoscience, environmental science and archaeological science, addressing questions across time and space.  The team values curiosity, inclusion and impact, supporting flexible work and professional growth in a collaborative environment. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .  Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.  Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.  Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Brandon Mahan at brandon.mahan@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').  Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 6 December 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018485 - Laser Ablation, Automation and Digitisation Research Scientist - PD.docx JR-018485 - Laser Ablation, Automation and Digitisation Research Scientist - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Student-Services-Representative_JR-019448": {
      "title": "Student Services Representative",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term until December 2027 (Multiple roles available in 2 different teams)  Department/School: Student and Scholarly Services – Stop 1  Salary: UOM 4: AUD $80,400 - $85,328 p.a. plus 17% super  Frontline student-facing roles across service desk and contact centre / extensive training and development / collaborative, inclusive Stop 1 culture  Apply now to join our Stop 1 frontline teams and shape outstanding student experiences across multiple service channels.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.     The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   You will join Stop 1’s frontline teams, supporting coursework students across in-person, phone and email channels. You will help students navigate fees, enrolment, documentation and support services, resolving diverse enquiries at first contact. You will build broad university knowledge, customer service expertise and career pathways within Student and Scholarly Services.  What you will deliver:    Provide accurate, consistent information via in-person, phone and email, resolving enquiries at first contact where possible.  Manage follow-up for complex enquiries, collaborating with Student Services teams and adhering to practice guidelines.  Contribute to knowledge bases, service improvement initiatives and student-focused projects, events, workshops and service desks.  Liaise with University teams to connect students with appropriate support services and coordinated solutions.  Participate in training, mentoring and onboarding of new staff, sharing knowledge and modelling excellent customer service.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a tertiary qualification and or equivalent experience in customer service or administrative environments.  Demonstrate strong commitment to high-quality customer service in busy, frontline contact centre or service desk settings.  Use CRM and student systems confidently, alongside MS Word, Excel, Outlook and strong internet search skills.  Adapt quickly to changing priorities, learning new information and resolving enquiries at first contact wherever possible.  Handle sensitive situations with discretion, applying sound judgement and maintaining confidentiality at all times.  Communicate clearly and professionally in spoken and written English; education sector or call centre experience is advantageous.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Student and Scholarly Services   Admin and General Enquiries   As a Student Services Representative in Admin and General Enquiries, you'll provide hands-on assistance, guiding students to services and resolving queries via multiple service channels. You'll work closely with teams within Student Services and the wider University, learning continuously to improve your ability to assist students. As a first point of contact, you'll represent both the University of Melbourne and Stop 1.  Contact Centre   As a Student Services Representative in the Contact Centre, you'll assist students via phone and email, helping them access services and aiming to resolve student administrative issues at the first point of contact. You'll collaborate with Student Services and other University teams, continuously upskilling to provide accurate responses to student queries. As a key point of contact, you'll represent both the University of Melbourne and Stop 1.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au .     Application essentials:   Please submit one application only using the “Apply” button; this will be considered for both role types.  Applications submitted via email will not be accepted.  Visit  \"apply with us\"  to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.    Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, please refer to the Hiring Manager attached to the Position Description  For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').     Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: Monday, 2nd of November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:   PD_JR-019448 Student Services Representative.pdf   Hiring Manager: Laura Cesile ( laura.cesile@unimelb.edu.au )   PD_JR-019441 Student Services Representative, Contact Centre.pdf   Hiring Manager: Anber Celik ( anber.celik@unimelb.edu.au )"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Enterprise-Professor-Fellow---Infrastructure-and-Transport-Platform_JR-017552": {
      "title": "Enterprise Professor/Fellow - Infrastructure and Transport Platform",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.6 FTE), Fixed-term for 2 years Faculty: Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology Department: Office of the Dean Salary: Level D: $183,859 - $202,556 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% super Level E: $236,824 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% super High-profile, industry-facing academic role / Bridge infrastructure and transport sectors with FEIT research / Shape impactful strategic partnerships. Apply your senior sector experience to build influential collaborations that advance infrastructure, transport, and university research impact. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead industry engagement for FEIT’s Infrastructure and Transport Platform, matching sector needs with world-class research capabilities. You will use your networks and executive experience to shape strategic partnerships that deliver commercial value and societal impact. You will also mentor platform staff and support the Platform Leader to grow a vibrant, industry-connected ecosystem. What you will deliver:   Forge strategic partnerships between infrastructure and transport organisations and FEIT researchers to address complex sector challenges. Identify and scope collaboration opportunities, including proposals and white papers aligned with sector priorities and FEIT capabilities. Champion the Infrastructure and Transport Platform at industry events, strengthening visibility, reputation, and stakeholder relationships. Mentor and coach platform staff to build capability in industry engagement and partnership development. Advise and support the Platform Leader on business development strategy, pipelines, and resource focus for maximum impact. You may be a great fit if you:   Bring extensive infrastructure and/or transport sector leadership experience and strong credibility with senior industry stakeholders. Demonstrate a strong track record in business development, partnership creation, and delivering mutually beneficial commercial outcomes. Maintain a contemporary, influential professional network and confidently leverage relationships to achieve strategic objectives. Understand current and emerging research challenges, regulations, and compliance trends across infrastructure and transport sectors. Offer senior executive experience within public or private organisations, with proven ability to lead and mentor others. Meet the Level E criteria through eminent industry leadership and recognition, or be ready to progress from Level D. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology You will join the Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology, a national leader in engineering and IT education and research. Working within the Infrastructure and Transport Platform, you will collaborate with experts across engineering, construction, mobility, logistics, and asset management. The team partners with industry, government, and communities to tackle real-world challenges and deliver transformational impact aligned with FEIT 2035 and the University’s Strategy 2030. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position and to our recruitment process, email Shabon Nawabi at Shabon.nawabi@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Tuesday 24 November 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-017552 Enterprise Professor Fellow Infrastructure and Transport Platform Position Description.pdf JR-017552 Enterprise Professor Fellow Infrastructure and Transport Platform Position Description Accessible.doc"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Fellow-or-Senior-Research-Fellow--Indigenous-Health-Equity_JR-019470-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow or Senior Research Fellow, Indigenous Health Equity",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time 1 FTE OR Part Time (0.6FTE to 1.0FTE); Fixed term available for  2  years  Faculty: Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department/ School:  Melbourne School of Public Health - Onemda, Indigenous Health Equity Unit  Salary: Level B or Level C  Level B: $124,656 - $148,023 per annum (pro rata for part-time) plus 17% super   Level C: $152,695 - $176,065 per annum (pro rata for part-time) plus 17% super    Aboriginal-led research environment / tackle intergenerational trauma and perinatal wellbeing / advance Indigenous health equity nationally  Apply now to shape trauma-informed, culturally safe research that improves outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander families.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic).     The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    You will join an Aboriginal-led team advancing Indigenous health equity through impactful perinatal trauma and family wellbeing research. You will lead and support mixed-methods projects that improve support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander parents. You will strengthen your leadership, supervision and knowledge translation capabilities while working closely with communities and policy makers.   What you will deliver:    Lead and coordinate research on intergenerational trauma, perinatal wellbeing and strategies to improve Indigenous health equity.  Produce high-quality peer-reviewed publications and diverse knowledge translation outputs for academic, policy and community audiences.  Supervise and mentor junior staff and graduate students, guiding ethics, study design, project management and stakeholder engagement.  Contribute to teaching, curriculum development and seminars within the Indigenous Health Equity Unit and School.  Engage with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, services and policymakers to support trauma-informed, culturally safe practice.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a PhD or equivalent training in public health, epidemiology, allied health, psychology or related fields.  Bring research experience in public health and/or Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health, including translational activities.  Demonstrate experience working respectfully with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, organisations and service providers.  Apply strong quantitative and qualitative data collection and analysis skills, using relevant analytical and statistical software.  Manage complex research projects with excellent organisation, deadline management and evidence of effective team collaboration.  Communicate clearly in writing and verbally with diverse stakeholders across research, policy, clinical and community settings.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team – Melbourne School of Public Health   You will join Onemda in the Melbourne School of Public Health, focused on Indigenous wellbeing. The Indigenous Health Equity Unit leads innovative, community-driven research, teaching and policy translation to improve Indigenous health. You will collaborate with Aboriginal leaders and multidisciplinary experts in a supportive, culturally safe, community-partnered environment.   Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description. (maximum of 300 words for each criteria). Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria   Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process email Vanessa Phan at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​13 November 2026​ 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    PD_Identified_Research Fellow_Senior Research Fellow Indigenous Health Equity.docx   PD_Identified_Research Fellow_Senior Research Fellow Indigenous Health Equity.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Graduate-Researcher-Academic-Associate_JR-018684-1": {
      "title": "Graduate Researcher Academic Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nTeach and mentor CIS students while building your academic career alongside your graduate research. Role type: Part-time (0.2–0.3 FTE for full-time graduate researchers); Fixed-term 18–24 months, aligned with candidature Faculty: Engineering and Information Technology Department/School: School of Computing and Information Systems Salary: $88,228 p.a. (pro rata) plus 17% super Build your academic teaching profile alongside your PhD / Shape CIS student learning at scale / Access 30 hours of certified development Apply now to combine paid, secure academic work with impactful CIS teaching and structured professional development. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   As a Graduate Researcher Academic Associate, you will contribute directly to high-quality teaching in Computing and Information Systems. You will gain practical experience designing, delivering and supporting CIS subjects while completing paid, certified university teaching development. What you will deliver:   Design and deliver tutorials, workshops, labs and applied sessions using engaging, evidence-based pedagogical approaches. Support students through consultations, forums, revision sessions and timely responses to queries across assigned subjects. Contribute to assessment design, marking, feedback and academic integrity processes, including managing exam and assignment workflows. Administer subject delivery, including Canvas sites, submissions, extensions, remark requests and coordination with casual teaching staff. Maintain and support educational technologies such as Ed, FeedbackFruits, JupyterHub and Github to enable effective learning experiences. You may be a great fit if you:   Are currently enrolled in a FEIT graduate research degree with at least 18 months remaining in candidature. Bring strong disciplinary knowledge in one or more CIS subject clusters, such as programming, artificial intelligence, or cybersecurity. Demonstrate enthusiasm for university teaching and a commitment to educating, inspiring and supporting diverse student cohorts. Communicate clearly in written and spoken English, tailoring explanations for students with varied backgrounds and abilities. Manage your time effectively, balancing research responsibilities with teaching preparation, delivery, assessment and administration. Engage proactively in your own professional development and uphold the University’s values of respect, safety and inclusion. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team You will join the School of Computing and Information Systems, Australia’s top-ranked computing school, within the Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology. The School delivers world-class teaching and research across computer science, software engineering, information systems and human-computer interaction, with strong partnerships across industry and leading research centres. You will work alongside passionate educators and researchers, contribute to innovative CIS curricula, and help deliver an outstanding learning experience for a large, diverse student community. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the below Key Selection Criteria questions. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria . The below criteria should be a maximum of 3 pages. Demonstrated knowledge and technical capability in computing, with strong foundations in programming and demonstrated expertise in at least one relevant area such as artificial intelligence, cyber security, software engineering, information systems or related fields. Candidates should be able to apply their knowledge to practical problems and engage with the technologies, tools and programming environments used in teaching. Demonstrated ability, or strong potential, to teach and explain technical concepts clearly to students with different levels of experience, including supporting students in practical classes, troubleshooting code or technical problems, and helping students develop conceptual understanding rather than simply providing solutions. Demonstrated practical capability in developing, testing and supporting computing learning activities and infrastructure, which may include programming exercises, assessment tasks, sample solutions, automated testing or marking, version control, notebooks, virtual environments, learning platforms or other discipline relevant technologies. Demonstrated capacity to support high quality and equitable assessment and feedback, including applying marking criteria consistently, providing constructive feedback, identifying common student misconceptions, and supporting appropriate academic integrity practices in contemporary computing education, including where generative AI may be relevant. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively, reliably and inclusively within a teaching team, including communicating effectively with students and colleagues, managing competing responsibilities and deadlines, responding constructively to emerging teaching issues, and contributing to the ongoing improvement of subjects and the student learning experience. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Eduardo Oliveira at eduardo.oliveira@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   CIS GRAA - Position Description.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Director-of-Social-Work-Field-Education_JR-018875-2": {
      "title": "Director of Social Work Field Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full Time; Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/ School:  Department of Social Work, School of Health Sciences Salary: Level C - $152,695 to $176,065 OR Level D - $183,254 to $202,556 p.a  plus 17% super  Shape the future of social work field education / drive innovative practice-based learning / strengthen sector-wide partnerships Apply now to lead a collaborative team delivering outstanding student experiences and future-focused social work education. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   In this role, you will lead field education across our accredited Master of Social Work. You will design transformative placement experiences in partnership with students, educators and diverse community organisations. You will also build a distinctive research program advancing innovation in professional practice education. What you will deliver:   Provide strategic leadership for all field education activities within the Master of Social Work program. Design, deliver and continuously improve contemporary field education curriculum and subjects aligned with accreditation requirements. Oversee diverse, high-quality student placements, ensuring excellent supervision, learning outcomes and risk management. Lead, mentor and develop the field education team, fostering inclusive, collaborative and high-performing culture. Build and sustain partnerships, research projects and sector initiatives that enhance social work education nationally.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in social work or cognate discipline and AASW-eligible social work qualification. Bring at least five years’ professional social work practice experience across relevant fields or settings. Demonstrate leadership of academic or professional teams, fostering inclusive, collaborative cultures and high-quality field education. Excel in innovative teaching and curriculum design, particularly practice-based and field education initiatives. Develop and disseminate impactful research or scholarship in field education, with emerging or established national profile. Build productive partnerships with universities, professional bodies and community organisations to drive educational innovation. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team You will join the Department of Social Work in the School of Health Sciences, within the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences. Our academic team is committed to social justice, ethical practice and innovative, evidence-informed education. We work closely with an extensive network of community, health and human service partners across diverse practice settings. You will be supported to lead, collaborate and grow your academic career in a collegial, inclusive environment. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Milena Heinsch For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 5 November 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018875 Director of Social Work Field Education .docx JR-018875 Director of Social Work Field Education .pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Clinical-Sites-Metro--Regional/Senior-Lecturer--Medicine--Echuca-_JR-019026-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer, Medicine (Echuca)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.2 FTE); Fixed term to 31 December 2028 Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/ School:  Department of Rural Health – Echuca Clinical School Salary:  Level C: $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. (pro rata for part-time) plus 17% super p.a. Lead general medicine teaching in Echuca / collaborate closely with Echuca Regional Health / shape future rural medical practitioners Apply now to lead rural general medicine education and create outstanding learning experiences for Echuca-based MD students. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead and coordinate general medicine teaching for Rural Clinical School students based in Echuca. You will collaborate with Echuca Regional Health teams, support student learning, and contribute to impactful rural research. What you will deliver:   Plan, organise and deliver high quality general medicine teaching, tutorials and clinical learning activities for RCS students. Coordinate student placements, ward rounds and inpatient experiences in medicine with Echuca Regional Health medical and education teams. Orientate students to Echuca Regional Health wards and processes, and provide ongoing guidance on professional conduct and skills. Contribute to assessment design, in-term assessments and examinations, ensuring fair, transparent and standards-aligned student evaluation. Participate in departmental committees, RCS educator meetings, and relevant research activities, including supervising student research projects. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a medical qualification recognised in Australia plus Fellowship or equivalent recognised postgraduate clinical qualification. Demonstrate strong experience in clinical teaching, medical education and curriculum delivery within hospital-based general medicine settings. Build effective working relationships with medical practitioners, health professionals and university colleagues across regional and rural contexts. Show understanding of student needs and provide thoughtful academic guidance, feedback and counselling when required. Exhibit strong organisational and administrative skills, managing teaching schedules, placements and assessment tasks efficiently. Maintain active clinical practice at Echuca Regional Health and commit to ongoing development of medical education capabilities. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – You will join the Department of Rural Health within the Melbourne Medical School, based at Echuca Clinical School. The department delivers high quality health professional education and research in rural communities across Victoria. Working closely with Echuca Regional Health, the team strengthens the rural health workforce through immersive clinical training. You will collaborate within a supportive, values-driven culture that champions innovation, inclusion and meaningful community impact. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Steve Bismire by emailing steven.bismire@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 5 November 2026. 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019026 Senior Lecturer, Medicine (Echuca) PD.docx JR-019026 Senior Lecturer, Medicine (Echuca) PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Student-Adviser--Course-Planning_JR-018874": {
      "title": "Student Adviser, Course Planning",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time, Fixed-Term for 12 months  Department:  Student and Scholarly Services   Salary: UOM 5 $86,943 – $99,864 p.a. plus 17% super  Frontline student advising across all courses / strong team culture with extensive training / broaden your University-wide networks. Apply now to support diverse students in planning their studies and build a rewarding student services career. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity    You will provide tailored course planning advice that helps students make confident decisions about their study pathways. Working across in-person and virtual channels, you will develop deep expertise in University courses and student support. You will join a high performing team that values collaboration, innovation and continuous improvement. What you will deliver:   Provide accurate, holistic enrolment and course planning advice across all coursework degrees, in person and online. Use University systems and resources to resolve complex student enquiries, escalating issues and following through to resolution. Contribute to high quality information content, presentations and workshops for student and internal staff channels. Support training, coaching and knowledge sharing within the team to build collective course and subject expertise. Participate in events and peak service periods, managing time effectively to maintain agreed service standards. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a tertiary qualification and or equivalent combination of education and relevant customer service experience. Demonstrate strong commitment to quality customer service within busy, high demand frontline environments. Thrive in changing settings, showing initiative, flexibility and collaborative approaches to shared team challenges. Offer experience supporting diverse, multicultural client groups, ideally within complex service or education organisations. Use CRM, student systems and Microsoft Office confidently, with strong online research and information navigation skills. Resolve complex enquiries or cases, balancing accuracy, empathy and timely outcomes for students. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Stop 1   Stop 1 is the University’s primary student services centre, supporting coursework students across their study journey. You will join Student and Scholarly Services, a professional team that enables excellent teaching and learning outcomes. Our collaborative, inclusive culture values innovation, continuous improvement, extensive training and clear pathways for future career growth. Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .  Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.  Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.  Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Andrew Devine at adevine@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien Nguyen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').  Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close:  Thursday 29 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018874 Student Adviser, Course Planning.pdf"
